WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.090
- Good evening, everyone.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:07.230
I'm Noel Lenski.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:10.380
I teach classics and history here at Yale,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.600
and I am charged with the
honor and the pleasure

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.580
of introducing our
speaker for this evening,

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:18.540
Professor Clifford Ando of
the University of Chicago.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.340
I'd just like to begin
with a few words, however,

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.770
about the namesake of our event,

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.560
Michael Ivanovich Rostovtzeff.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.320
Tomorrow, the 10th of November,

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.810
will mark the 154th anniversary

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.090
of Rostovtzeff's birth in Zhytomyr,

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.550
in what is today southwestern Ukraine,

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.340
about 140 kilometers west of Kyiv,

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.340
therefore, a place troubled
greatly by violence

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:42.990
inflicted by the state of Russia,

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:45.390
a state in which Rostovtzeff grew up

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.633
and, in fact, was
educated under the Czars.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:51.720
He studied classics and ancient history

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.790
at Kyiv and St. Petersburg,

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:56.430
where he went on to serve
as professor of Latin

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.940
for 20 years down to 1918.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.600
In the upheaval of the Russian Revolution,

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.910
Rostovtzeff, who opposed the Bolsheviks,

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.530
was forced to flee Russia,

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:08.520
and eventually landed in
Madison, Wisconsin in 1920.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:10.170
After teaching there for five years,

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.620
he moved to New Haven to take
up a professorship at Yale,

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.000
and remained there down
to his retirement in 1944.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.110
He lived on until 1952, when
he passed away in New Haven,

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.210
and is now buried in the
Grove Street Cemetery

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:29.193
where a group of us went today
to lay a wreath on his tomb.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.550
Rostovtzeff was a brilliant scholar

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.950
who revolutionized the
study of the ancient world

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.220
in ways that have only
really come into clear focus

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.200
in contemporary America,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.090
the contemporary world at the moment.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.250
He combined an interest in archeology

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.410
and in archeological field work

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:49.980
with a passion for economic
and cultural history.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.457
His two greatest works,

00:01:51.457 --> 00:01:53.970
"The Social and Economic
History of the Roman Empire,"

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.130
published first in 1926,

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.650
and "The Social and Economic History

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.870
of the Hellenistic
World", published in 1941,

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.330
were both gold mines of information

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:06.570
produced well before the
information revolution.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.550
A voracious reader of countless languages

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.610
and an avid aficionado
of quantitative methods,

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:12.990
he set the benchmark

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:15.360
for source-based historical investigations

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.990
in the second quarter of the 20th century.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:21.240
Rostovtzeff published some 12 monographs

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.440
and countless articles

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.410
displaying a breathtaking
knowledge of ancient history,

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.170
from early Greece through late Rome,

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.700
and from Africa to Gaul, to
Egypt, to the Crimea, to Syria.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:33.900
It was his field work

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.990
that uncovered the fortress
city of Dura-Europos,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.550
parts of which are now on display

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.010
in the Yale University Art Gallery.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.200
Although his methods fell out of favor

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.640
in the mid-20th century,

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.010
in large part due to
the unmistakable imprint

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.680
left by his bitter experience
of the Russian Revolution

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.480
on his scholarly views,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.090
Rostovtzeff's taste for big data

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.000
and for the intermingling of
textual and material evidence

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.240
have come roaring back in
the scholarly presence.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.250
In addition to his scholarly works,

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:05.760
his excavation finds and his
methodological innovations,

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.320
Rostovtzeff continues to impact

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:11.460
Yale's intellectual environment
through this lecture series.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.550
The annual Michael Rostovtzeff
lecture and colloquium

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.370
was made possible through
a bequest by his widow,

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.190
the late Sophie Kulczycki Rostovtzeff.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:21.750
For the last 16 years,

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:22.800
we've been able to welcome

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.450
some of the most distinguished researchers

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.220
in the field of ancient history

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.170
as lecturers in the series,

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:31.020
and to sponsor a symposium
the following morning

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.030
including equally distinguished scholars

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.290
to comment on the lecture

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.660
and engage in learned
dialogue with the lecturer.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.340
So that will occur tomorrow morning,

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:43.340
beginning at 9:00 AM in Phelps Hall 401.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.500
Our speaker this evening, Clifford Ando,

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.810
is the David B. and Clara F. Stern

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.820
distinguished service professor.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.340
He's professor of classics and history

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.440
at the University of Chicago,

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.790
where he is chair and has
been for the last six years.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.650
Ando studied at Princeton University

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:03.330
and then at the University of Michigan,

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.210
where he completed his PhD in 1996.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.647
He's the editor of the monographic series,

00:04:08.647 --> 00:04:09.870
"Empire and After,"

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:11.730
with the University of Pennsylvania Press,

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.670
senior editor of the Bryn
Mawr Classical Review,

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:18.270
editor of "KNOW: A Journal on
the Formation of Knowledge,"

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:21.330
and is on the editorial
board of seven other journals

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:23.250
or monographic series.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.020
He has held visiting professorships

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:27.270
or temporary appointments
at 14 universities

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.840
across the United States,
Europe, and New Zealand.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:32.580
He has held numerous fellowships,

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:34.800
including the ACLS Burkhardt Fellowship,

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.830
and currently, an NEH Scholarly Editions

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:39.240
and Translations grant

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.030
for his project on Roman statutes,

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:43.980
which, I believe, is the source

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.770
for some of what he'll
talk about this evening.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:48.720
Ando is the author of seven monographs,

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:51.030
including "Imperial Ideology
and Provincial Loyalty

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:52.260
in the Roman Empire,"

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.900
published with the University
of California Press in 2000,

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:58.380
which won the Charles Goodwin
Award of Merit in 2003

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:02.310
from the then American
Philological Association.

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:04.380
He's the editor of 14 books,

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:08.130
including "The Oxford Handbook
of Roman Law and Society,"

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:10.447
and the first volume of the forthcoming

00:05:10.447 --> 00:05:12.090
"Cambridge World History of Rights,"

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:14.040
which will appear next year.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.040
He's published over 120
articles and 60 reviews

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.360
and short notes.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:21.690
He is, in brief, boundless in energy,

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:25.950
but also in range and in brilliance.

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:27.090
Just as to range,

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:29.190
Ando covers in his published research

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:31.680
fields as diverse as the Roman Republic,

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.560
Roman imperial history, late antiquity,

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.350
Latin and Greek literature,
the history of law,

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.260
the history of religion,
the history of philosophy,

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:43.080
and the philosophy of law,
the philosophy of language,

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:44.400
and the history of knowledge.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:45.723
And I could go on.

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:49.320
His work has had a major
impact on the field

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:50.640
in at least four respects.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:53.820
First and foremost, Ando
has played a crucial role,

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.590
arguably the crucial role,

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:57.510
in bringing classics and ancient history

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:59.370
into dialogue with philosophy,

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:01.380
particularly the philosophy of mind,

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.050
epistemology, and phenomenology.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.930
This was clear already
in his first monograph,

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:09.540
which covered a remarkable range of time

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:11.100
and breadth of sources,

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.470
and situated these into
a sociological framework

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:16.440
constructed especially
around the Frankfurt School

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:19.233
and his theory of law and
reason within the life world.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.310
Second, Ando has helped make
classics and ancient history

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:26.670
a serious interlocutor with
scholars of law and government.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:29.490
His recent work on citizenship
in the ancient world,

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:31.170
particularly in the Roman world,

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:34.020
has helped us to rethink
our contemporary world,

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:36.870
where questions of citizenship
and state belonging

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.270
are deeply relevant and deeply implicated

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:42.900
in discourses of inclusion and exclusion.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.710
Third, Ando's serious work on language,

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.870
not just at the level of philology,

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:50.610
a field in which he is expert,

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:51.510
but more importantly,

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.370
in the realm of philosophy of language,

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.470
have helped us to comprehend the way

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:58.170
in which linguistic habits and practices

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:00.330
shape the way that we think and reason,

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:03.213
and thus, the way that we shape our world.

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:07.470
But finally, his work as
a coordinator of people,

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.300
of ideas, of information,

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.690
make him a hub around which
so much that is happening

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:15.630
in ancient history, legal
history, and intellectual history

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:17.520
is turning at the moment.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.470
The project on Roman
statutes, for example,

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.960
seeks to bring together all
the evidence that we have

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:25.860
for positive law in the Republic
and early imperial periods,

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:28.770
a task as gargantuan as it is frustrating,

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:30.240
given the tantalizing

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:33.060
but fragmentary nature of the material.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:34.410
I think it's fair to say

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:36.750
that there is no other
scholar working today

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:39.360
with the breadth of
expertise and connections

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.190
to make this project work.

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:43.410
Without Clifford Ando in our world,

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:46.350
the study of ancient
history, of antiquity,

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.600
of the history of
knowledge and of religion

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:51.030
would be greatly impoverished,

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:53.910
which is why it brings me
great pleasure this evening

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:56.310
to be able to offer
you a chance to partake

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:59.130
in the riches that he has to offer,

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:03.300
to enjoy the learning and
the breadth of knowledge

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.280
that Professor Ando can bring to us.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:07.170
And so, without further ado,

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:10.380
I would ask us all to join
in welcoming Clifford Ando

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:12.427
to speak to us tonight on the subject of

00:08:12.427 --> 00:08:16.696
"Empire's Law: The Roman State
in the Second Century BCE."

00:08:16.696 --> 00:08:19.196
(all applaud)

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:47.670
- So thank you, Noel, for that
quite amazing introduction.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:50.400
So I'm gonna try to be disciplined today,

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.180
'cause I realized that a
version of this lecture

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:56.760
was sent to the people

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.920
who are gonna lead the
conversation tomorrow.

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:01.620
And I'm gonna have to
suppress my natural urge

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:05.820
just to, like, run off and improvise,

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:07.170
because I think it's most fair

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:08.910
to the people who've actually
already read the text

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:10.440
and are gonna be responding to the text

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:11.590
that they have received

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:16.143
to adhere closely to the document.

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:20.651
I'll try to be good in a
way that I'm often not.

00:09:20.651 --> 00:09:23.550
So I want to start also, in
addition to thanking Noel,

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:25.170
to thank the Department of Classics

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:27.063
for this extraordinary invitation.

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:31.920
I spent a great deal of
time in graduate school

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.530
reading the prestige
works of ancient history,

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:37.260
the way you will as a kind
of person entering a field.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:38.460
So I spent a great deal of time

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:42.240
reading Simon, Brunt, and Miller,

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:45.480
and then Hopkins, or Finley
and Hopkins, I should say.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:49.230
And then, you know, Eck,
and Nicolet, and so on,

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:50.250
all of whom have something like

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:53.490
a substantial and legible pedigree

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:55.140
worthwhile reflecting on at this moment

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:55.973
because the chairs of

00:09:55.973 --> 00:09:57.420
the Roman history chair in Oxford

00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:59.280
and the chair of ancient
history at Cambridge

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:00.570
are vacant at the same time,

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:02.793
I think for the first time ever.

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:05.610
And I also spent a lot of time

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:08.613
reading Rostovtzeff and MacMullen,

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:11.280
who did not, so far as I could tell,

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:12.780
as far as I could piece them together,

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:14.430
have a pedigree that I could understand.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:16.200
I couldn't understand
their educational histories

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:17.310
or why they wrote what they wrote

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:19.200
in a way that is sort
of intelligible to me

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:21.150
why the other people did what they did.

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:25.590
I worked on the past on
topics that align closely

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:28.590
with central themes in Rostovtzeff's work.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:31.140
Today's lecture, I confess, does not,

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:33.390
but I want to pay tribute, as I begin,

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:35.610
to the quite extraordinary
sequence of ancient historians

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:36.443
who've taught here.

00:10:36.443 --> 00:10:38.490
And that includes not simply Rostovtzeff

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:41.193
and Ramsay MacMullen, whom I miss.

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:47.160
I've spent a lot of time in
sitting in the Atticus Cafe

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.577
being told by Ramsay something like,

00:10:48.577 --> 00:10:50.047
"Well, you get everything wrong."

00:10:50.047 --> 00:10:51.930
(audience laughs)
When you,

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:53.880
almost all conversations
begin with something like

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:55.950
Ramsay remarking on a
paper I'd recently sent in

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:56.783
by saying something like,

00:10:56.783 --> 00:10:58.380
"Cliff, when you say 'X,'"

00:10:58.380 --> 00:10:59.213
or frequently, he'd say,

00:10:59.213 --> 00:11:02.732
"When you say, 'The Romans say,'
I wanna know which Romans."

00:11:02.732 --> 00:11:03.900
And then...
(audience laughs)

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:05.700
I'm sure many of you had
conversations like this

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.140
with Ramsay McMullen.

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:11.040
And, of course, also John
Matthews and Noel himself,

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:13.380
who also works in many
fields of ancient history

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.480
and, I think, brings a distinct respect

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:16.890
both for evidence and method

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:19.353
to all the fields in which he works.

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:21.743
Oh, wait.

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:25.353
I think I have to move the cursor.

00:11:26.419 --> 00:11:27.423
(gasps) There we go.

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:29.603
How exciting.

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:34.020
So what I'm going to do today

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:38.790
is start by talking a
little bit about some,

00:11:38.790 --> 00:11:40.260
I think, characteristic myths

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:42.753
that undergird the study of Roman law.

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:46.240
In a written version of this,

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:48.180
I talk about more of them,

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:49.440
if only because

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:52.110
sometimes taking out a
kind of a truism of a field

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:54.420
and holding it up to the light is,

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:57.363
well, I mean, if you're a
nerd, kind of hilarious.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:01.800
Especially people outside
the field of Roman law

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:03.240
could well ask themselves, you know,

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:04.925
as a kind of anthropological matter,

00:12:04.925 --> 00:12:07.110
did you realize you were in a university

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:09.260
with people who believe
this sort of thing?

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:12.000
And then I'm gonna spend
a little bit of time,

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.190
as you will in any ancient history talk,

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:15.960
in making a set of empirical claims

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:20.163
about what evidence we
actually have for something.

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:23.280
And then I'm going to
talk about the moment

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:25.680
when something like the
vernacular narrative,

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:28.080
which I'm going to try to
disprove, came into being,

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:29.370
and talk briefly about

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:32.400
why people believe what
they do about Roman law,

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:34.920
before, in the end, making a
whole bunch of bravura claims

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.950
about what actually happened
in the second century Republic,

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:39.753
which, as I was remarking to Seth,

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:45.540
are, up to a point, disconnected
from my empirical claims,

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:47.370
and have the great
virtue by being so broad,

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:48.390
as broad as they are,

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:50.460
of being, I think, probably not provable,

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:51.930
but not falsifiable either,

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:54.423
which is a, you know, a
good thing for a lecture.

00:12:56.280 --> 00:13:01.280
So let me just start, then,
by talking briefly about

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:04.470
what I take to be two discrete myths

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:07.083
that undergird vernacular
histories of Roman law.

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:12.480
The first, the Romans produced
complex written statutes

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:16.143
in a continuous tradition
from at least 510 BCE,

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:21.240
and second, prior to the development

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:23.550
of the so-called formulary system,

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:26.340
the civil law was
administered by pontiffs,

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:28.620
but probably also by magistrates,

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:31.560
positive law rested on the Twelve Tables,

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:34.680
but procedure had been specified
by subsequent statutes,

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:36.570
except that these
statutes seems themselves

00:13:36.570 --> 00:13:37.560
to have been laid out,

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:40.770
to have laid out the positive
law that they regulated.

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:42.093
I'll take these in turn.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:48.450
First, regarding the history
of Roman legislation,

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:51.030
there have been several
attempts to catalog,

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:52.143
mostly from Livy,

00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:55.080
Roman laws chronologically,

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:56.850
of which the most
influential modern effort

00:13:56.850 --> 00:13:59.070
is that by Giovanni Rotondi.

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:02.040
After a simply phenomenal
and extended introduction

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:05.010
that covers all aspects of
the production of legislation,

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:08.760
Rotondi offers lists of
statutes according to genre,

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:13.593
as here, section two,
laws de provocazione,

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:17.700
followed by a catalog

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:21.420
that provides for each
item, ancient testimonia,

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:24.750
a quick summary of the
law's supposed content,

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:27.300
followed by references to research tools

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:30.003
on matters of detail and context.

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:34.980
The format of the modern
catalog by Marianne Elster

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:35.880
is very similar,

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:38.640
albeit her representation
of the data is much fuller.

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:40.740
It's in that sense that Elster's work

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:42.963
is much closer to a standalone resource.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:47.340
To give you some idea of
the scale of the enterprise

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:50.490
as it is mapped by these
modern research tools,

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:54.210
Marianne Elster catalogs 231 laws

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:56.250
of the so-called middle Republic alone,

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:59.973
from 366 to 134 BCE.

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:07.710
The tiny print's not a
deliberate act of ambiguation.

00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:11.010
None of this data is
relevant to what will follow,

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:12.090
except in if you were writing

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:15.270
some sort of history of the Roman novel.

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:16.420
We'll get back to that.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.900
Second, regarding the
history of Roman civil law,

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:25.500
in the first chapters of book
four of his "Institutes,"

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:28.080
Gaius offers a brief idiosyncratic history

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:30.093
of some aspects of Roman procedure.

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:34.080
In particular, Gaius
describes five legal actions

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:37.383
that he terms "legislaciones,"
statutory actions,

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:40.233
as follows.

00:15:41.407 --> 00:15:43.410
"The actions that the ancients had in use

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:45.990
were called statutory actions,

00:15:45.990 --> 00:15:48.120
either because they were
created by statutes,

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:50.070
since, of course, the
edicts of the praetor,

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:52.290
by which many actions had been introduced,

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:54.330
were not yet in use,

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:56.070
or because they were framed in the words

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:57.210
of the statutes themselves

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:59.190
and were therefore treated as immutable,

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:00.783
even as the statutes were.

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:04.740
Whence it was held that a man who,

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:06.690
when suing for the
cutting down of his vines,

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:09.240
used the word 'vines' in his claim,

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:12.810
lost his case, because he
ought to have said 'trees,'

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:14.700
seeing that the law of the Twelve Tables,

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:17.730
on which his action for
cutting down his vines lay,

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:20.937
spoke of cutting down trees in general."

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:25.440
Now Gaius is more or less our only source

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:27.000
for pre-formulary procedure,

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:29.613
and his account is as
partial as it is tantalizing.

00:16:30.930 --> 00:16:33.630
It's a cautionary tale
about both our knowledge,

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:35.040
but also their knowledge of law

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:36.990
before the second century BCE,

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:38.850
that we know next to nothing about the law

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:40.650
that principally affected the change

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:44.640
from the statutory actions
to the formulary procedure.

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:47.520
I refer to what the Romans
called the lex Aebutia,

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:48.930
date unknown,

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:51.277
about which Rotondi himself says,

00:16:51.277 --> 00:16:52.710
"To this law is attributed

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:54.750
the introduction of formulary procedure,

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:57.510
but it is impossible to
determine its precise content

00:16:57.510 --> 00:16:58.737
or its impact."

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:03.303
Now I will close,

00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:05.940
I'll say more before I close

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:08.580
about the lex Aebutia
and the Twelve Tables,

00:17:08.580 --> 00:17:11.340
but in brief, my claim
is that Gaius received

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:13.830
two traditions about early Roman law.

00:17:13.830 --> 00:17:15.450
One concerned the Twelve Tables,

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:18.720
the other concerned the
so-called statutory actions.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:21.620
What we'd witnessed in the
opening paragraphs of book four

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:24.810
is his attempt to allow for
both of these traditions

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:26.523
to be operative at the same time.

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:29.410
We should not believe either.

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:33.750
Instead, in order to understand
what the Romans were doing

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:36.420
when they invented themselves
as creatures of law,

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:38.280
we need to abandon our own longing

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:40.350
for knowledge that we cannot have,

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:43.260
which has induced a kind of credulity

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:46.027
that we would tolerate in
no other field of inquiry.

00:17:46.027 --> 00:17:48.990
"We," meaning services,
especially historians of law,

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:51.813
not the good citizens of classics.

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:54.540
And examined instead the context

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:57.540
in which these Roman
speculations were made.

00:17:57.540 --> 00:17:59.253
More on this theme as we go along.

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:04.470
And finally, before I turn
to the substance of my talk,

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:06.300
I want to pay tribute to what I think is

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:07.860
one of the greatest books of Roman history

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:09.360
of the 20th century,

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:12.177
namely, Arnold Toynbee's
"Hannibal's Legacy."

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:15.720
Today, if Toynbee is cited at all,

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:17.820
it is as an exemplar of error

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:20.433
in regard to the history
of land use in Italy,

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:24.570
but this was not a major
concern for Toynbee.

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:25.920
On the contrary,

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:29.070
his interest lay in the
form of the Roman state,

00:18:29.070 --> 00:18:31.650
and his thesis, simply stated,

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:33.760
might be that Roman hegemony in Italy

00:18:34.866 --> 00:18:36.990
prior to the first Punic war,

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:38.940
took a specific form,

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:41.550
distinctive both within
the ancient Mediterranean

00:18:41.550 --> 00:18:43.620
and, indeed, within the
history of federal structures

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:45.810
known in the world at the time.

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:47.430
The wars with Carthage,

00:18:47.430 --> 00:18:50.670
Rome's engagement with the
Hellenistic world more generally,

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:52.440
brought Rome into competition

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:54.930
with states of a very different form.

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:56.970
And in order to beat them,

00:18:56.970 --> 00:18:59.160
and in consequence of defeating them,

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:01.770
Rome, itself, was transformed.

00:19:01.770 --> 00:19:05.040
In short, power existing
in memetic relation

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:06.990
to that which it resists.

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:09.360
Through competition
with Hellenistic powers,

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:12.450
Rome itself became a Hellenistic kingdom.

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:15.570
And yet, it preserved both
public law structures in Italy

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:18.240
and republican forms of
politics domestically

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:21.900
that were increasingly at odds
with the scale of state power

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:24.513
and the political economics
of Mediterranean empire.

00:19:25.717 --> 00:19:28.470
"Hannibal's Legacy" traces
the history of this dissonance

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:30.360
and its effects on myriad aspects

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:33.330
of Roman social and economic relations.

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:36.090
This is not the place to
assess Toynbee's achievement,

00:19:36.090 --> 00:19:37.560
but I should like at the very least

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:41.340
to insist that one give
his argument its due.

00:19:41.340 --> 00:19:44.343
It was not simple, nor was it falsifiable.

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:46.800
The conclusion of this paper

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:48.180
will try to take up the challenge

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:49.683
that he laid before the field.

00:19:54.900 --> 00:19:59.193
So the evidence that
we have for Roman law,

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:01.650
part one, statute.

00:20:01.650 --> 00:20:04.230
And Noel is of course completely correct

00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:05.820
that I was inspired to this project

00:20:05.820 --> 00:20:07.920
by essentially tracing out patterns

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:10.800
and reading, and rereading,
and rereading, and rereading

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:13.503
the corpus of epigraphically
preserved Roman laws.

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:18.600
Now is it, and I've
done that work for you,

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:20.168
so none of you has to.

00:20:20.168 --> 00:20:21.870
(audience laughs)

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:24.617
So you're welcome.

00:20:24.617 --> 00:20:26.520
(audience laughs)

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:28.860
It's a truism of scholarship
on Roman legal thought,

00:20:28.860 --> 00:20:30.990
and on Roman culture more generally,

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:32.970
that the jurists were
like their compatriots

00:20:32.970 --> 00:20:35.013
in having a deep respect for history.

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:38.850
I've written along these lines myself,

00:20:38.850 --> 00:20:41.520
with a caveat that I've
argued that the jurists,

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:43.410
and Roman theorists more generally,

00:20:43.410 --> 00:20:46.122
consistently assembled
historical data in order to argue

00:20:46.122 --> 00:20:49.503
recent practice has precedence
over hoary antiquity.

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:54.360
That is why, as each successive
generation of jurists

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:56.970
wrote commentaries on
earlier commentaries,

00:20:56.970 --> 00:20:58.590
on earlier commentaries,

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:02.313
it became unnecessary to
cite the words of statute.

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:06.930
Actions at law were available
in respect of statutes

00:21:06.930 --> 00:21:10.170
based on recent acts of
application and interpretation,

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:13.980
and these were largely available
in contemporary language.

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:16.200
The contrast with Athens,

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:18.510
where the wording of the original statute

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:20.880
had to be cited in court,

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:22.060
could not be stronger

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:28.800
Despite this interpretive position

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:30.180
with regard to the Roman jurists

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:31.860
and their respect for the past,

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:34.350
even a conventional history
like that of Aldo Schiavone,

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:36.120
so everyone else insisted,

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:38.010
I mean, basically all of the scholars

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:39.750
think that Roman jurists
and the Romans in general

00:21:39.750 --> 00:21:41.130
like to preserve the past.

00:21:41.130 --> 00:21:44.490
It's something about mos
maiorum, crap like that.

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:49.260
And despite our insistence
that the Romans valued the past

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:51.300
and they valued precedent and so on,

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:54.270
even people like Aldo
Schiavone has to admit

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:57.330
that their knowledge of
academic legal writing

00:21:57.330 --> 00:21:58.950
before the 90s BCE

00:21:58.950 --> 00:22:00.843
was close to non-existent.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:06.337
He says, "Despite," I'll
start with the second.

00:22:06.337 --> 00:22:07.957
"Despite this," I'll
skip the first paragraph.

00:22:07.957 --> 00:22:10.470
"Despite this tendency

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:12.660
of the whole course of
republican jurisprudence

00:22:12.660 --> 00:22:14.100
until Quintus Mucius,

00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:17.763
later jurors would preserve
only a vague and blurry memory.

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:19.890
In the body of legal literature

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:21.300
that survived to the present day,

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:24.510
the theses and doctrines of
authors prior to Quintus Mucius

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:26.100
are explicitly cited on no more

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:27.720
than about 15 occasions at all,

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:29.850
and very few of these references

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:32.847
suggest a direct reading
of the original texts."

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:38.460
My ambition today is to
reveal a similar pattern

00:22:38.460 --> 00:22:40.140
with regard to knowledge

00:22:40.140 --> 00:22:44.100
by persons in the second
century BCE of earlier law,

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:45.390
and then to pose some questions

00:22:45.390 --> 00:22:47.007
about the conclusions we might draw

00:22:47.007 --> 00:22:49.290
and the speculations we might make

00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:50.793
on the basis of this pattern.

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:55.920
Now I've never read a book or an article

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:57.750
that reflects on the historical data

00:22:57.750 --> 00:22:59.820
about the history of Roman legislation

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:03.663
that is contained within
the legislation itself,

00:23:04.650 --> 00:23:09.570
but the writers of Roman laws
knew a lot about Roman law.

00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:12.510
They understood that any
given piece of legislation

00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:14.640
intervened in a landscape,

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:16.770
a social world that was regulated by

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:20.193
and conformed itself to prior legislation.

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:26.610
So for example, the first
extant Roman statute,

00:23:26.610 --> 00:23:31.610
the lex repetundarum of
122 BCE cites three laws.

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:34.660
The lex Rubria is very
likely this statute of 122

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:38.790
that authorized the foundation
of the colony of Carthage

00:23:38.790 --> 00:23:41.250
and is therefore Gracchan in date.

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:44.400
The lex Calpurnia is a law
on magisterial malfeasance

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:46.470
of 149 BCE,

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:48.240
and the lex Junia is unknown

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:50.520
outside two references to it in this text,

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:53.130
but the fact that it's cited
after the lex Calpurnia

00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:55.473
strongly suggests that
it postdates that law.

00:23:57.090 --> 00:23:59.760
In short, the lex repetundarum

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:03.573
cites no law earlier than 149 BCE.

00:24:07.110 --> 00:24:08.850
The picture is similar

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:11.973
if we turn to the lex agraria of 111 BCE.

00:24:12.870 --> 00:24:15.240
Beyond some generic
references to situations

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:17.550
that were created by prior
enactments of the people

00:24:17.550 --> 00:24:19.560
in lines 15 and 29,

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:23.520
the lex agraria makes reference
to four named statutes.

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:25.050
Oop, yeah.

00:24:25.050 --> 00:24:29.880
A lex Sempronia, a lex
Rubria, a lex Livia,

00:24:29.880 --> 00:24:32.859
and a censorial law of
Lucius Caecilius Metellus

00:24:32.859 --> 00:24:35.253
and Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus.

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:40.980
The lex Sempronia de
agraria dates to 123 BCE,

00:24:40.980 --> 00:24:42.540
namely, a law of Gaius Gracchus

00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:44.610
that, in large measure, reactivated a law

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:47.850
of his brother Tiberius
from a decade earlier.

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:50.340
The lex Rubria on the
foundation of Carthage dates,

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:53.070
as we have seen, to 122 BCE.

00:24:53.070 --> 00:24:55.680
The lex Livia is the law of 146 BCE

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:57.270
that appointed a senatorial commission

00:24:57.270 --> 00:25:00.870
to assist Scipio Aemilianus
in the organization of Africa.

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:02.289
And Lucius Caecilius Metellus

00:25:02.289 --> 00:25:03.750
and Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus

00:25:03.750 --> 00:25:05.943
were censors in 115 BCE.

00:25:08.130 --> 00:25:11.610
In other words, the lex
agraria cites no statute

00:25:11.610 --> 00:25:14.073
earlier than 146 BCE.

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:18.780
The pattern holds,

00:25:18.780 --> 00:25:21.270
if one continues the
analysis in this form,

00:25:21.270 --> 00:25:25.530
throughout the entire corpus
of existing Roman statutes.

00:25:25.530 --> 00:25:28.230
I cannot promise that I
caught every citation,

00:25:28.230 --> 00:25:29.790
but the ones relevant to this project

00:25:29.790 --> 00:25:32.084
are probably that in the
lex de provinciis praetoriis

00:25:32.084 --> 00:25:33.630
of 100 BCE,

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:36.870
referring to a law of Marcus
Porcius Cato of unknown date,

00:25:36.870 --> 00:25:39.540
but almost, very, very
likely contemporaneous

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:41.670
with the law on praetorian
provinces itself,

00:25:41.670 --> 00:25:43.880
and a citation of a so-called lex Laetoria

00:25:43.880 --> 00:25:45.543
in the tablet of Heraclea.

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:48.330
Now, we have a number of later references

00:25:48.330 --> 00:25:50.443
to something called the lex Laetoria.

00:25:50.443 --> 00:25:52.920
A law of the same name
is cited by Plautus,

00:25:52.920 --> 00:25:54.480
which seems anomalous.

00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:55.920
The content of the lex Laetoria

00:25:55.920 --> 00:25:58.230
cited in chapter 84 of
the Flavian municipal law

00:25:58.230 --> 00:25:59.340
is so different,

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:00.526
that Michael Crawford has speculated

00:26:00.526 --> 00:26:03.783
that the name used in the
tablet of Heraclea is a mistake.

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:06.900
Now. bracketing this problematic case,

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:11.040
the pattern in the totality of
subsequent legislation holds.

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:13.920
No certain reference is ever made

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:15.750
in an extant Roman legislation

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:20.100
to a statute passed before 149 BCE,

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:21.660
and no reference at all

00:26:21.660 --> 00:26:25.410
can even implausibly be reconstructed

00:26:25.410 --> 00:26:28.743
so as to refer to a
statute before 200 BCE.

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:35.730
You can probably tell where
I'm going with this argument.

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:37.770
My claim is going to be

00:26:37.770 --> 00:26:41.340
that no one before the
1st century, give or take,

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:45.270
ever mentions a statute written
before the Hannibalic War.

00:26:45.270 --> 00:26:47.460
Nor reports, of course,
if you don't cite it,

00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:49.710
you can't report the
words of such a statute.

00:26:50.550 --> 00:26:52.233
But let me proceed step by step.

00:26:55.680 --> 00:26:59.640
The next genre of evidence I
want to take up is oratory.

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:01.800
Although I focus on reports about orators

00:27:01.800 --> 00:27:03.240
of the second century BCE,

00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:05.670
I should say a word about
the few reports we have

00:27:05.670 --> 00:27:07.830
about earlier writers.

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:09.750
Substantively it makes
no difference to my case,

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:10.890
since the reported fragments

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:12.640
have almost nothing to do with law.

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:17.910
But in the sole case that does or might,

00:27:17.910 --> 00:27:20.730
that is to say, this
fragment of Censorinus,

00:27:20.730 --> 00:27:24.570
not only does 250 years separate the event

00:27:24.570 --> 00:27:27.330
from its first so-called testimonial,

00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:28.710
but the anecdote itself,

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:31.230
supposedly on the occasion
of his holding the censorship

00:27:31.230 --> 00:27:34.290
for a second time, around 80 years of age,

00:27:34.290 --> 00:27:35.793
is completely implausible.

00:27:36.870 --> 00:27:38.913
That said, so I mean,

00:27:38.913 --> 00:27:42.870
so I think it's clear that the
entire event is just made up.

00:27:42.870 --> 00:27:44.040
That neither of the people,

00:27:44.040 --> 00:27:46.530
neither Valerius Maximus nor Plutarch

00:27:46.530 --> 00:27:48.540
knew anything about this.

00:27:48.540 --> 00:27:50.070
But that isn't even important.

00:27:50.070 --> 00:27:53.520
What is interesting is that
the elaboration of the legend

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:56.340
does illustrate a pattern
that interests me.

00:27:56.340 --> 00:27:57.690
For what it's worth,

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:00.060
the story as it's related
by Valerius Maximus

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:02.643
reports only a speech before the people.

00:28:05.280 --> 00:28:07.113
But two generations later,

00:28:08.100 --> 00:28:10.650
the story had become one about public law,

00:28:10.650 --> 00:28:12.540
hence, in Plutarch, right?

00:28:12.540 --> 00:28:14.193
They voted, he passed a law.

00:28:15.930 --> 00:28:18.450
The two testimonia thus illustrate

00:28:18.450 --> 00:28:21.960
a pattern of very considerable
historical importance,

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:25.110
for all that the
testimonia amount to lies.

00:28:25.110 --> 00:28:26.640
And they are lies.

00:28:26.640 --> 00:28:28.290
It's clear neither Valerius Maximus

00:28:28.290 --> 00:28:30.450
nor Plutarch had a reliable source.

00:28:30.450 --> 00:28:33.180
They are not individually
drawing on a tradition

00:28:33.180 --> 00:28:36.300
that reported a contio
and the passage of a law,

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:39.990
such that Valerius Maximus
selectively mentions the assembly

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:41.610
and Plutarch mentions the law,

00:28:41.610 --> 00:28:43.350
the sorts of arguments that people make

00:28:43.350 --> 00:28:45.063
using shitty evidence like this.

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:49.950
On the contrary, the Romans
came to want to see themselves

00:28:49.950 --> 00:28:53.970
as a people whose life had been
regulated by law ab initio.

00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:55.560
And in each retelling,

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:58.983
the past came to resemble the
story that the Romans needed.

00:28:59.970 --> 00:29:02.610
Why this occurred and when it occurred

00:29:02.610 --> 00:29:05.053
is an important historical explanandum.

00:29:08.130 --> 00:29:10.620
Now allow me to be precise
about what I'm arguing

00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:13.470
about the evidence of the second century.

00:29:13.470 --> 00:29:15.240
It is not that political oratory

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:18.270
did not discuss matters of law.

00:29:18.270 --> 00:29:20.700
The fragments of the
second century orators

00:29:20.700 --> 00:29:22.563
frequently concern legislation,

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:26.610
but none of the orators is represented,

00:29:26.610 --> 00:29:29.220
none of them is ever represented, I think,

00:29:29.220 --> 00:29:33.300
as arguing for or against
the passage of a law,

00:29:33.300 --> 00:29:34.140
or, for that matter,

00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:38.250
arguing for or against a
particular political decision

00:29:38.250 --> 00:29:41.820
or for or against any policy decision

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:45.900
by means of the citation of
archaic or republican law,

00:29:45.900 --> 00:29:47.670
mid-republican law.

00:29:47.670 --> 00:29:51.210
Indeed, none of them ever
names an historical statute,

00:29:51.210 --> 00:29:53.160
let alone regards a contemporary problem

00:29:53.160 --> 00:29:55.710
as potentially regulated by earlier law,

00:29:55.710 --> 00:29:57.870
or even analogous to a situation

00:29:57.870 --> 00:30:00.723
that might be potentially
regulated by earlier law.

00:30:04.050 --> 00:30:06.510
It might be useful to situate this pattern

00:30:06.510 --> 00:30:08.400
in respect of legal history

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:09.600
within a broader pattern

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:12.330
of both attitudes to
history and normativity

00:30:12.330 --> 00:30:14.730
and within kind of ways
of talking about history

00:30:15.874 --> 00:30:17.583
and normativity at the time.

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:23.993
I think this slide offers

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:32.220
the sum total of references to custom,

00:30:32.220 --> 00:30:35.730
to one's betters or the
greaters, i.e., the maiores,

00:30:35.730 --> 00:30:38.190
and the full roster of exemplary stories

00:30:38.190 --> 00:30:41.493
in second century oratory
down to about 120.

00:30:43.650 --> 00:30:47.160
Which is not to say the
later literature we have

00:30:47.160 --> 00:30:48.870
does not attribute relevant usage

00:30:48.870 --> 00:30:51.390
to texts of the second century BCE,

00:30:51.390 --> 00:30:53.760
but when it's sort Jelius, for example,

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:55.050
that a second century orator

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:56.790
is made to speak of his betters,

00:30:56.790 --> 00:30:58.920
I think we should be as
suspicious as when Jelius

00:30:58.920 --> 00:31:02.100
caused Scipio Africanus
to use the term imperium

00:31:02.100 --> 00:31:03.600
to refer to empire.

00:31:03.600 --> 00:31:06.720
That is if other things about the,

00:31:06.720 --> 00:31:07.680
in the case of Jelius,

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:10.020
when Jelius attributes to
people in the second century

00:31:10.020 --> 00:31:13.380
language that is simply totally
implausible in one sentence,

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:15.900
I think we should be deeply suspicious

00:31:15.900 --> 00:31:17.190
about the rest of the fragment.

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:19.490
There's more we could
discuss here, obviously.

00:31:22.620 --> 00:31:25.380
Now, before I proceed with the rest of,

00:31:25.380 --> 00:31:28.710
before I proceed to the
next part of the paper,

00:31:28.710 --> 00:31:31.350
I wanna offer a caveat regarding evidence

00:31:31.350 --> 00:31:33.690
I'm not gonna survey publicly,

00:31:33.690 --> 00:31:35.160
to which I'm gonna append a caution

00:31:35.160 --> 00:31:36.630
about the content of that evidence,

00:31:36.630 --> 00:31:39.603
namely, the so-called fragments
of the Roman historians.

00:31:41.070 --> 00:31:42.060
Now, it may well happen

00:31:42.060 --> 00:31:44.580
that the content of one or
another fragment of an historian

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:48.570
is going to destabilize the
empirical basis of my project,

00:31:48.570 --> 00:31:50.670
but I think some caution is in order here.

00:31:51.630 --> 00:31:53.760
Very few fragments of the Roman historians

00:31:53.760 --> 00:31:56.373
contain the actual words
of earlier writers.

00:31:57.900 --> 00:32:00.420
More importantly, Romans
of nearly every period

00:32:00.420 --> 00:32:04.200
wrote on the assumption of a
kind of homeomorphy over time

00:32:04.200 --> 00:32:05.940
at the level of both practices

00:32:05.940 --> 00:32:07.563
and institutional arrangements.

00:32:08.460 --> 00:32:11.700
This was part of the
point of writing history

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:15.390
in the form of lists, like Fosti.

00:32:15.390 --> 00:32:17.220
They both organized memory

00:32:17.220 --> 00:32:19.617
and they asserted a kind
of unspoken continuity

00:32:19.617 --> 00:32:21.843
in institutional operations.

00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:24.660
What is taken for granted

00:32:24.660 --> 00:32:27.180
when we say that we have
always elected consuls

00:32:27.180 --> 00:32:29.523
and, indeed, that we know who they are?

00:32:32.340 --> 00:32:36.510
Roman historians were
therefore not historicist.

00:32:36.510 --> 00:32:39.360
Their intuitions will have
been to assume continuity

00:32:39.360 --> 00:32:42.543
along more axes of social
practice than we would do.

00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:47.340
In consequence, Roman historians
cite earlier historians

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:50.460
largely as it regards points
of agreement or disagreement

00:32:50.460 --> 00:32:52.590
on matters of detail,

00:32:52.590 --> 00:32:54.725
because each generation is
taken by later generations

00:32:54.725 --> 00:32:57.600
to have inhabited the
same Rome as they did,

00:32:57.600 --> 00:32:59.670
and their narratives are
represented in their texts

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:01.713
are paraphrased on this assumption.

00:33:02.670 --> 00:33:04.260
If a historian of the late Republic

00:33:04.260 --> 00:33:06.930
or an Augustan historian
cites an earlier analyst

00:33:06.930 --> 00:33:09.210
as if that person narrated Roman history

00:33:09.210 --> 00:33:10.440
as a history of laws,

00:33:10.440 --> 00:33:12.690
one's gonna have to be
very careful, I think,

00:33:13.708 --> 00:33:17.220
in assessing how much
agreement, and of what kind,

00:33:17.220 --> 00:33:18.570
in regarding what matters

00:33:18.570 --> 00:33:20.673
is being asserted by that citation.

00:33:23.370 --> 00:33:26.760
Finally, it matters to the
broader claim I'm making

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:28.200
that historians in the ancient world

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:32.280
were generally not conditioned
to see absence as evidence.

00:33:32.280 --> 00:33:33.990
In reading their predecessors,

00:33:33.990 --> 00:33:35.880
they will have been triggered by patterns

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:38.583
other than those that
demand our attention.

00:33:44.250 --> 00:33:45.423
But we are not they,

00:33:47.100 --> 00:33:48.540
and so we are free to ask,

00:33:48.540 --> 00:33:50.490
I think we are called upon to ask,

00:33:50.490 --> 00:33:53.460
whether the absence of evidence
in this particular case

00:33:53.460 --> 00:33:55.470
is evidence of something.

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:57.210
What does it reveal?

00:33:57.210 --> 00:33:59.810
And what is entailed by our
answer to that question?

00:34:01.650 --> 00:34:03.540
So far as I can tell,

00:34:03.540 --> 00:34:07.260
no writer in the second century
BCE found it useful to cite

00:34:07.260 --> 00:34:09.840
or even to refer to written legislation

00:34:09.840 --> 00:34:12.240
that originated prior to
the second century BCE.

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:18.000
Despite this silence on the
part of authors, jurists,

00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:22.050
and orators for the duration
of the second century,

00:34:22.050 --> 00:34:24.690
the fulsome catalogs
of earlier legislation

00:34:24.690 --> 00:34:28.260
that one can compile
from later literature,

00:34:28.260 --> 00:34:30.390
from Cicero, Livy, and so on,

00:34:30.390 --> 00:34:33.033
demand that we believe
something like the following.

00:34:34.260 --> 00:34:37.050
Throughout the second century BCE,

00:34:37.050 --> 00:34:41.280
secure data regarding the long
history of Roman legislation

00:34:41.280 --> 00:34:44.280
was available and reliable.

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:47.820
It simply went uncited by anyone,

00:34:47.820 --> 00:34:50.010
but was accurately preserved.

00:34:50.010 --> 00:34:52.350
And then it was recovered
in the age of Sulla,

00:34:52.350 --> 00:34:55.050
or whenever it was that
we begin to see it cited.

00:34:55.050 --> 00:34:57.150
And when this, whenever this occurred,

00:34:57.150 --> 00:35:00.570
the historical data were
reported in a fulsome

00:35:00.570 --> 00:35:02.163
and disinterested fashion.

00:35:04.260 --> 00:35:06.450
This picture of the
transmission of knowledge

00:35:06.450 --> 00:35:10.320
is exactly as plausible as the
data that Augustus recovered

00:35:10.320 --> 00:35:12.636
when he himself did research,

00:35:12.636 --> 00:35:13.560
(audience laughs)

00:35:13.560 --> 00:35:15.570
and read the speech of
Metellus Macedonicus

00:35:15.570 --> 00:35:17.430
on the duty to produce children,

00:35:17.430 --> 00:35:19.710
or found linen books in
the recesses of temples

00:35:19.710 --> 00:35:22.233
containing rules on the
celebration of triumphs.

00:35:23.490 --> 00:35:25.290
I'll say a bit more about
these kind of episodes

00:35:25.290 --> 00:35:27.573
in Roman scholarship later.

00:35:29.880 --> 00:35:32.160
Now, a minimalist conclusion
that we might draw

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:33.990
from the patterned silence in our sources,

00:35:33.990 --> 00:35:36.570
to wit, from the absence
of evidence for earlier law

00:35:36.570 --> 00:35:38.430
during the second century BCE,

00:35:38.430 --> 00:35:40.800
is that no written legislation survived

00:35:40.800 --> 00:35:43.140
from the earlier period
into the second century.

00:35:43.140 --> 00:35:46.320
And this is why titles are
known in the first century,

00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:48.783
but, more or less, no language.

00:35:50.940 --> 00:35:55.050
For my part, I urge a maximalist position.

00:35:55.050 --> 00:35:57.990
Written legislation from before 200 BCE

00:35:57.990 --> 00:35:59.483
was not known in the second century

00:35:59.483 --> 00:36:02.073
because it never existed
in the first place.

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:08.310
Institutions of communal
decision making surely existed.

00:36:08.310 --> 00:36:10.020
The assembly made decisions,

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:13.113
but these were, by and
large, not written down.

00:36:17.100 --> 00:36:18.660
Now, it would take some time to lay out

00:36:18.660 --> 00:36:20.970
all the grounds for
skepticism that one might cite

00:36:20.970 --> 00:36:22.710
regarding the knowledge
that the Romans had

00:36:22.710 --> 00:36:23.610
of their own past.

00:36:23.610 --> 00:36:25.260
And I should stress, you know,

00:36:25.260 --> 00:36:28.590
that lots and lots of people
have written beautifully

00:36:28.590 --> 00:36:31.200
and at length about the
reasons to be skeptical

00:36:31.200 --> 00:36:34.803
about the history of the
early and middle Republic,

00:36:35.760 --> 00:36:38.760
and at some level the
field oscillates over time

00:36:38.760 --> 00:36:40.920
in 20 or 25 year increments

00:36:40.920 --> 00:36:43.770
about just how much credulity
we extend to this information.

00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:46.320
I'm not the first person
to compile these passages.

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:52.380
So I should add, right, I should insist

00:36:52.380 --> 00:36:55.590
all modern historians are
aware of the extent to which

00:36:55.590 --> 00:36:58.740
their sources have been
exposed as chimerical.

00:36:58.740 --> 00:37:01.290
I think we just oscillate
in our unwillingness

00:37:01.290 --> 00:37:03.903
to draw the conclusions
that our methods demand.

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:08.340
So let me cite only the
most famous cautions

00:37:08.340 --> 00:37:11.280
offered to us by the ancients themselves.

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:12.870
I commence with Cicero's "Brutus,"

00:37:12.870 --> 00:37:14.820
discussing the legends
invented and rehearsed

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:16.503
within aristocratic houses.

00:37:18.307 --> 00:37:19.530
"And yet these eulogies

00:37:19.530 --> 00:37:22.620
have made the record of Rome's
history more unreliable,

00:37:22.620 --> 00:37:25.440
since they include many
things that did not happen.

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:27.810
False triumphs, exaggerated
numbers of consulships,

00:37:27.810 --> 00:37:30.840
even false genealogies and
transfers to the plebs,

00:37:30.840 --> 00:37:32.280
merging people of lower standing

00:37:32.280 --> 00:37:34.170
with another family of the same name,

00:37:34.170 --> 00:37:37.230
as though I were to claim
descent from the Manius Tullius

00:37:37.230 --> 00:37:39.536
who was a patrician consul
with Servius Sulpicius

00:37:39.536 --> 00:37:42.597
in the 10th year after the
expulsion of the kings."

00:37:45.930 --> 00:37:48.690
When I say, "the legends
invented and rehearsed

00:37:48.690 --> 00:37:50.670
within aristocratic houses,"

00:37:50.670 --> 00:37:51.503
I mean, of course,

00:37:51.503 --> 00:37:53.550
something like the
totality of raw material

00:37:53.550 --> 00:37:56.733
available to the historical
tradition as they understood it.

00:38:00.210 --> 00:38:03.000
Alongside this, one might
cite Livy's digression

00:38:03.000 --> 00:38:05.760
on the reliability of
evidence available to him,

00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:07.650
which runs along very similar lines

00:38:07.650 --> 00:38:09.720
as he confronted problematic data

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:11.493
in the history of the Samnite Wars.

00:38:12.607 --> 00:38:14.520
"Nor is it easy to prefer one narrative

00:38:14.520 --> 00:38:16.440
or one authority over another.

00:38:16.440 --> 00:38:18.660
Historical memory has been
contaminated, I think,

00:38:18.660 --> 00:38:20.580
by funeral orations

00:38:20.580 --> 00:38:23.310
and lying inscriptions
beneath portrait busts

00:38:23.310 --> 00:38:25.020
as every family through deceitful lying

00:38:25.020 --> 00:38:26.700
appropriates to itself high offices

00:38:26.700 --> 00:38:28.100
and noteworthy achievements.

00:38:29.160 --> 00:38:30.450
Certainly it's from this cause

00:38:30.450 --> 00:38:32.463
that the deeds of individuals
and records of public actions

00:38:32.463 --> 00:38:34.470
have been confused.

00:38:34.470 --> 00:38:35.790
Nor does any writer survive

00:38:35.790 --> 00:38:37.230
who is contemporary with these times

00:38:37.230 --> 00:38:41.907
on whom one might reasonably
rely as a reliable source."

00:38:47.040 --> 00:38:49.050
General statements like these

00:38:49.050 --> 00:38:51.240
should be read alongside
Livy's repeated cautions

00:38:51.240 --> 00:38:54.720
that even in regard to second
century events and personages,

00:38:54.720 --> 00:38:56.400
the accounts and evidence available to him

00:38:56.400 --> 00:38:58.470
were in radical disagreement.

00:38:58.470 --> 00:38:59.843
And we're now talking about
Livy on the second century.

00:38:59.843 --> 00:39:03.153
This is not Livy on some
episode in hoary antiquity.

00:39:04.657 --> 00:39:06.030
"Many other things concerning

00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:07.950
particularly the end of Scipio's life,

00:39:07.950 --> 00:39:10.740
his trial, his death, funeral, and tomb

00:39:10.740 --> 00:39:12.303
pull in opposite directions,

00:39:13.320 --> 00:39:14.970
so that I do not know which report

00:39:14.970 --> 00:39:16.680
or which writer I should follow.

00:39:16.680 --> 00:39:18.330
There is no agreement as the accuser.

00:39:18.330 --> 00:39:19.800
Some write that Marcus Naevius,

00:39:19.800 --> 00:39:21.810
others that the Petillii accused him.

00:39:21.810 --> 00:39:24.840
There's no agreement about the
timing, nor about the trial,

00:39:24.840 --> 00:39:26.280
nor about the year when he died,

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:29.160
nor about where he died
or where he was buried.

00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:31.080
Some say he died and was buried at Rome,

00:39:31.080 --> 00:39:32.580
others at Liternum.

00:39:32.580 --> 00:39:35.817
In both places, monuments
and statues are on display."

00:39:36.750 --> 00:39:38.887
This isn't the effing body of Orestes.

00:39:38.887 --> 00:39:40.350
(all laugh)

00:39:40.350 --> 00:39:42.240
This is a person who lived
in real historical time,

00:39:42.240 --> 00:39:44.303
and they haven't got a
clue where his body is.

00:39:46.710 --> 00:39:48.300
Livy is similarly at a loss

00:39:48.300 --> 00:39:50.820
regarding the death of Claudius Marcellus,

00:39:50.820 --> 00:39:53.340
where he follows Coelius
Antipater at least far enough

00:39:53.340 --> 00:39:56.250
to say that the account
left by Marcellus' own son,

00:39:56.250 --> 00:39:58.800
who is an eyewitness
to his father's death,

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:00.460
was implausible.

00:40:00.460 --> 00:40:03.750
(audience laughs)

00:40:03.750 --> 00:40:06.063
Frankly, may be true, but.

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:10.140
But perhaps the most important
such remark on Livy's part

00:40:10.140 --> 00:40:12.540
concerns the supposed
prosecution of Africanus

00:40:12.540 --> 00:40:13.910
for which Livy had the evidence

00:40:13.910 --> 00:40:16.230
of a fake speech attributed to Africanus

00:40:16.230 --> 00:40:18.280
and a fake speech attributed to Gracchus.

00:40:19.747 --> 00:40:23.070
"Nor is the disagreement only
among writers of history.

00:40:23.070 --> 00:40:24.780
The speeches too,

00:40:24.780 --> 00:40:26.760
if they are in fact what
they are said to be,

00:40:26.760 --> 00:40:29.670
namely, those of Publius
Scipio and Tiberius Gracchus,

00:40:29.670 --> 00:40:31.860
are inconsistent with each other.

00:40:31.860 --> 00:40:33.540
The title of the speech of Publius Scipio

00:40:33.540 --> 00:40:36.300
has the name of Marcus Naevius
the tribune as accuser.

00:40:36.300 --> 00:40:38.670
The speech itself is without
the name of the accuser.

00:40:38.670 --> 00:40:41.313
Now it calls him a
scoundrel, now a trifler.

00:40:42.210 --> 00:40:44.177
Nor does even Gracchus'
speech make any mention at all

00:40:44.177 --> 00:40:46.740
of the Petilii as accusers of Africanus

00:40:46.740 --> 00:40:48.577
or of the prosecution," blah, blah, blah.

00:40:48.577 --> 00:40:51.210
"An entirely different
story must be put together,

00:40:51.210 --> 00:40:53.100
consistent with Gracchus' speech,

00:40:53.100 --> 00:40:55.863
and those writers must
be followed," and so on.

00:40:58.440 --> 00:41:00.930
In other words, the extant
speeches are taken by Livy

00:41:00.930 --> 00:41:02.250
as serious evidence

00:41:02.250 --> 00:41:04.770
despite having no plausible authenticity.

00:41:04.770 --> 00:41:07.860
The speech of Gracchus, I
think, is clearly a declamation.

00:41:07.860 --> 00:41:10.320
But Livy then tells you that he accepts it

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:13.740
as the plausible document
among this array,

00:41:13.740 --> 00:41:16.050
and he allows that it's
going to be the north star

00:41:16.050 --> 00:41:18.843
that shapes how he writes
his narrative of the event.

00:41:21.000 --> 00:41:22.920
Regarding the specific
lessons we might draw

00:41:22.920 --> 00:41:24.480
from these remarks and
others in this vein,

00:41:24.480 --> 00:41:26.340
I can do no better than
quote the reprimand

00:41:26.340 --> 00:41:29.100
that Fergus Miller offered
to mid-republican historians

00:41:29.100 --> 00:41:30.153
a generation ago.

00:41:31.590 --> 00:41:33.630
I look out at republican historians

00:41:33.630 --> 00:41:35.700
feeling like I'm just
doing what Miller did.

00:41:35.700 --> 00:41:38.760
I'm like, I wandered into
republican history from the empire

00:41:38.760 --> 00:41:40.020
and I'm saying stupid stuff.

00:41:40.020 --> 00:41:42.370
But I'm gonna read out
what Fergus said anyhow.

00:41:43.747 --> 00:41:46.560
"We tend to insist not
only on knowing too much,

00:41:46.560 --> 00:41:48.780
but on knowing precisely those things

00:41:48.780 --> 00:41:51.990
which the ancient sources
themselves in their better moments

00:41:51.990 --> 00:41:53.910
warn us that we cannot know.

00:41:53.910 --> 00:41:57.390
That is to say, the attribution
to dateable individuals

00:41:57.390 --> 00:41:59.310
of particular actions and achievements

00:41:59.310 --> 00:42:00.720
and the grouping of individuals

00:42:00.720 --> 00:42:03.153
into supposedly reliable family trees.

00:42:09.150 --> 00:42:11.790
Now, regarding what I have
termed "their sources,"

00:42:11.790 --> 00:42:12.723
in quotes,

00:42:16.350 --> 00:42:18.180
we must always remember

00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:22.380
that the Romans saw fit to
build a standard set of fosti,

00:42:22.380 --> 00:42:25.680
a continuous record of
holders of high office,

00:42:25.680 --> 00:42:28.653
only in the last decade
or so of the Republic.

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:32.280
The infrastructure to date things

00:42:32.280 --> 00:42:34.830
even in the most rudimentary terms,

00:42:34.830 --> 00:42:36.900
which perforce amounted to the data

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:39.450
by which laws might be
named and placed in time,

00:42:39.450 --> 00:42:41.580
because of the way the Romans named laws,

00:42:41.580 --> 00:42:43.380
did not exist in vernacular form

00:42:43.380 --> 00:42:46.080
until after Cicero wrote "De re Publica."

00:42:46.080 --> 00:42:47.880
Because as Cicero wrote "De re Publica,"

00:42:47.880 --> 00:42:49.200
he was still writing to Atticus

00:42:49.200 --> 00:42:52.020
to verify consular dates
about events in that work,

00:42:52.020 --> 00:42:53.457
as also in the "Brutus."

00:42:54.330 --> 00:42:56.640
The existence of a
complete list of consuls

00:42:56.640 --> 00:42:58.710
was more than a condition of possibility

00:42:58.710 --> 00:42:59.790
for the form of the state

00:42:59.790 --> 00:43:01.590
that the Romans claim for themselves

00:43:02.490 --> 00:43:03.360
and the kind of narrative

00:43:03.360 --> 00:43:05.160
through which they told their story.

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:09.720
There.

00:43:09.720 --> 00:43:10.553
Ah, that's weird.

00:43:10.553 --> 00:43:13.830
It changed on the one screen
before it changed on the other.

00:43:13.830 --> 00:43:16.440
The fleshing out of piecemeal knowledge

00:43:16.440 --> 00:43:19.260
by the attribution of fictive consulates

00:43:19.260 --> 00:43:21.480
to the same gentes over and over again

00:43:21.480 --> 00:43:25.263
is frankly why we have laws
in duplicate or triplicate,

00:43:26.280 --> 00:43:27.113
right?

00:43:28.140 --> 00:43:30.730
It is emphatically not true

00:43:32.010 --> 00:43:35.250
that the Valerii had a fondness for,

00:43:35.250 --> 00:43:37.740
were, quote unquote, "known for,"

00:43:37.740 --> 00:43:40.050
sponsoring laws de provocatione,

00:43:40.050 --> 00:43:41.070
which is the sort of bullshit

00:43:41.070 --> 00:43:43.293
you're frequently told in Roman histories.

00:43:44.250 --> 00:43:46.980
In the literature, the
existence of the laws

00:43:46.980 --> 00:43:48.960
is taken to establish an association

00:43:48.960 --> 00:43:51.240
between the family and the cause,

00:43:51.240 --> 00:43:54.870
which association is then
cited in pure circularity

00:43:54.870 --> 00:43:57.243
to defend the reality of any one law.

00:43:58.230 --> 00:43:59.930
But this is completely ridiculous.

00:44:02.940 --> 00:44:05.760
The laws were
straightforwardly a byproduct

00:44:05.760 --> 00:44:07.713
of the invention of the fosti.

00:44:09.952 --> 00:44:11.943
Once you actually wrote down the fosti,

00:44:13.800 --> 00:44:16.740
in something like the 40s BCE, right?

00:44:16.740 --> 00:44:18.540
And there is no evidence that
they had any written record

00:44:18.540 --> 00:44:22.770
of any fosti before the
second century BCE, right?

00:44:22.770 --> 00:44:23.640
Once these were,

00:44:23.640 --> 00:44:26.700
once you populated your
made up list with Valerii,

00:44:26.700 --> 00:44:28.237
the answer to the question,

00:44:28.237 --> 00:44:30.000
"Which Valerius was it that proposed

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:33.030
a lex Valeria de
provocatione?" was obvious.

00:44:33.030 --> 00:44:34.113
Each of them did.

00:44:37.950 --> 00:44:39.600
Now, it's precisely for this reason

00:44:39.600 --> 00:44:42.960
that it was during the lifetime
of Cicero, or just before,

00:44:42.960 --> 00:44:45.480
that we begin to see
inklings of the legal culture

00:44:45.480 --> 00:44:47.133
that will flourish thereafter.

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:50.820
Commencing, perhaps,
from the age of Numantia,

00:44:50.820 --> 00:44:54.510
narratives begin to appear
of the middle Republic

00:44:54.510 --> 00:44:57.420
that contain doublets of later events.

00:44:57.420 --> 00:45:00.000
Meaning that a past was being invented,

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:02.430
or at least was then being narrated

00:45:02.430 --> 00:45:04.350
in such a way that the language and norms

00:45:04.350 --> 00:45:06.300
relevant to some contemporary issue

00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:08.610
were being discovered in the past,

00:45:08.610 --> 00:45:12.750
which thereby became available
as norm in the present.

00:45:12.750 --> 00:45:16.645
A treaty made in usu
populi is not binding.

00:45:16.645 --> 00:45:18.806
(speaking Latin)

00:45:18.806 --> 00:45:21.420
and all these kind of things
in contemporary language

00:45:21.420 --> 00:45:24.170
that are then attributed to
people of the mid-Republic.

00:45:26.790 --> 00:45:29.730
This material aggregates
in an accelerating way

00:45:29.730 --> 00:45:32.040
and is finally granted
unassailable prestige

00:45:32.040 --> 00:45:33.420
and normative power

00:45:33.420 --> 00:45:35.370
when Augustus discovered,

00:45:35.370 --> 00:45:38.190
in the so-called republican
constitutionalism,

00:45:38.190 --> 00:45:40.380
by which I mean late, late, late,

00:45:40.380 --> 00:45:42.570
late republican constitutionalism,

00:45:42.570 --> 00:45:44.253
the foundations of monarchy.

00:45:45.090 --> 00:45:47.340
It was during the reign of
Augustus and not earlier

00:45:47.340 --> 00:45:50.100
that parties on all sides
seized on public law

00:45:50.100 --> 00:45:52.770
as the lens through
which to construct a past

00:45:52.770 --> 00:45:55.230
that was continuous with the present,

00:45:55.230 --> 00:45:58.740
we were always already
a polity ordered by law,

00:45:58.740 --> 00:46:01.710
by way of establishing a
new language of politics

00:46:01.710 --> 00:46:03.873
for the age of republican monarchy.

00:46:05.796 --> 00:46:07.620
The debates that issued in the misnamed

00:46:07.620 --> 00:46:09.720
lex de imperio Vespasiani

00:46:09.720 --> 00:46:11.910
and the records that are preserved,

00:46:11.910 --> 00:46:13.290
the records of votes that are preserved

00:46:13.290 --> 00:46:15.300
in the commentary of the Arval Brethren

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:16.893
are the best evidence of this.

00:46:18.420 --> 00:46:21.420
The same might be said of
these researchers of Augustus

00:46:21.420 --> 00:46:23.730
that I cited earlier.

00:46:23.730 --> 00:46:24.630
That is to say,

00:46:24.630 --> 00:46:26.130
his claim that he discovered a speech

00:46:26.130 --> 00:46:28.550
of the censor Quintus Caecilius Metellus

00:46:28.550 --> 00:46:30.783
on the duty to marry and bear children.

00:46:33.570 --> 00:46:35.400
Now, before I make some
concluding remarks,

00:46:35.400 --> 00:46:37.950
a word is in order
about the Twelve Tables.

00:46:37.950 --> 00:46:40.200
The one early law whose words,

00:46:40.200 --> 00:46:43.080
or, I should say, some of whose words,

00:46:43.080 --> 00:46:46.173
Romans of the first
century BCE claim to know.

00:46:47.160 --> 00:46:48.273
Now, I'm skeptical.

00:46:49.897 --> 00:46:51.750
For one thing, as we've seen,

00:46:51.750 --> 00:46:54.300
several Romans tell us
that their own knowledge

00:46:54.300 --> 00:46:55.980
of events before the Hannibalic War

00:46:55.980 --> 00:46:57.303
were deeply insecure.

00:46:58.140 --> 00:47:01.110
And all Romans repeatedly
demonstrate the truth of this

00:47:01.110 --> 00:47:02.820
through sheer not citing,

00:47:02.820 --> 00:47:04.650
and apparently not even knowing,

00:47:04.650 --> 00:47:07.110
any words of any document
outside of treaties

00:47:07.110 --> 00:47:09.753
from the first 500 years of the Republic,

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:12.483
except for the Twelve Tables.

00:47:13.590 --> 00:47:17.880
Which, as a preview for tomorrow,

00:47:17.880 --> 00:47:20.055
Seth told me I may be
wrong about this, but,

00:47:20.055 --> 00:47:21.060
(audience laughs)

00:47:21.060 --> 00:47:26.060
which I think goes uncited
for at least 250 years

00:47:26.280 --> 00:47:27.630
before suddenly,

00:47:27.630 --> 00:47:29.937
deeply secure knowledge
of the Twelve Tables,

00:47:29.937 --> 00:47:33.783
and no intervening
document, suddenly emerges.

00:47:35.280 --> 00:47:36.600
Now, if there's an argument to be made

00:47:36.600 --> 00:47:38.190
for the existence of the Twelve Tables,

00:47:38.190 --> 00:47:40.410
it would have to center
on later narratives

00:47:40.410 --> 00:47:42.810
of something like early
hermeneutic projects,

00:47:42.810 --> 00:47:46.140
in particular, the
"Tripartita" of Sextus Aelius,

00:47:46.140 --> 00:47:48.270
which might suggest that
something called the Twelve Tables

00:47:48.270 --> 00:47:53.130
already existed or was
invented circa 200 BCE.

00:47:53.130 --> 00:47:55.650
But if even this much
is true, Aelius failed,

00:47:55.650 --> 00:47:57.750
because no author for
the next hundred years

00:47:57.750 --> 00:47:59.850
cited either the Twelve Tables

00:47:59.850 --> 00:48:02.133
or, for that matter, Aelius' own work.

00:48:05.010 --> 00:48:06.630
Two remarkable scholarly projects

00:48:06.630 --> 00:48:09.210
have been devoted to the
Twelve Tables in recent years.

00:48:09.210 --> 00:48:10.890
The one led by Floreana Corsi

00:48:10.890 --> 00:48:13.350
and the stunning edition
of Michel Humbert.

00:48:13.350 --> 00:48:16.140
I don't wish rashly to
discount their work,

00:48:16.140 --> 00:48:19.020
but I think we need more
than the testimony of Cicero

00:48:19.020 --> 00:48:21.780
that the Tables was a necessarium carmen,

00:48:21.780 --> 00:48:24.090
alongside, rather oddly,

00:48:24.090 --> 00:48:27.540
a reference to the legislation
of Solon, of all things,

00:48:27.540 --> 00:48:29.400
to prove that this was not in essence

00:48:29.400 --> 00:48:31.170
a collective invention of many parties

00:48:31.170 --> 00:48:33.300
of the late third century BCE,

00:48:33.300 --> 00:48:35.370
if not, as I happen to think,

00:48:35.370 --> 00:48:37.353
an invention of the age of Numantia.

00:48:41.850 --> 00:48:43.890
So I conclude with remarks on,

00:48:43.890 --> 00:48:46.110
quick remarks on three topics,

00:48:46.110 --> 00:48:50.040
legal history, their
sciences of history and ours,

00:48:50.040 --> 00:48:52.443
and the Roman state in
the second century BCE.

00:48:54.690 --> 00:48:56.140
Now, regarding legal history,

00:48:57.210 --> 00:48:59.100
no Roman statute,

00:48:59.100 --> 00:49:01.890
nor practically any document
produced and authorized

00:49:01.890 --> 00:49:04.140
by the institutional
machinery of the Roman state

00:49:04.140 --> 00:49:06.060
survives in a contemporaneous copy

00:49:06.060 --> 00:49:08.160
from before the end of the Hannibalic War.

00:49:09.180 --> 00:49:10.440
Later Roman writers,

00:49:10.440 --> 00:49:14.250
which is to say, Roman
writers writing much later,

00:49:14.250 --> 00:49:16.410
say that such statutes existed,

00:49:16.410 --> 00:49:19.380
but they cite neither the
language of such statutes,

00:49:19.380 --> 00:49:21.600
nor do they provide a plausible account

00:49:21.600 --> 00:49:24.060
of any institution of
knowledge transmission

00:49:24.060 --> 00:49:27.690
that would explain how they
got their supposed knowledge.

00:49:27.690 --> 00:49:29.493
I suggest to you they did not exist.

00:49:30.810 --> 00:49:35.040
Virtually nothing, perhaps
in actuality nothing

00:49:35.040 --> 00:49:36.660
of what the later literary sources say

00:49:36.660 --> 00:49:40.440
about the history of law before
the Hannibalic War is true.

00:49:40.440 --> 00:49:43.290
Certainly, there is no
evidence of any kind

00:49:43.290 --> 00:49:45.000
that corroborates their claims,

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:47.130
nor any plausible evidence for a mechanism

00:49:47.130 --> 00:49:49.530
by which knowledge was
preserved and passed down

00:49:49.530 --> 00:49:52.470
to suggest how authors in
the later first century BCE

00:49:52.470 --> 00:49:54.690
might have learned of such documents.

00:49:54.690 --> 00:49:57.723
In believing them, we give
our credence to legend.

00:50:00.150 --> 00:50:03.390
In support of this claim, I've
cited the writers of statute.

00:50:03.390 --> 00:50:04.260
They were, themselves,

00:50:04.260 --> 00:50:06.780
practitioners of a kind of legal history,

00:50:06.780 --> 00:50:09.850
and they cite no law before 149 BCE

00:50:12.180 --> 00:50:14.280
To put the matter another way,

00:50:14.280 --> 00:50:16.830
I suggest to you that
the pattern of survival

00:50:16.830 --> 00:50:19.260
regarding evidence for
written Roman statutes

00:50:19.260 --> 00:50:21.150
is not happenstance.

00:50:21.150 --> 00:50:23.043
Rather, the history,

00:50:23.970 --> 00:50:26.850
our extant corpus of inscribed Roman laws,

00:50:26.850 --> 00:50:29.730
reflects the actual history
of legislative enactment

00:50:29.730 --> 00:50:30.783
fairly accurately.

00:50:33.060 --> 00:50:34.800
In this light, what is
perhaps most stunning

00:50:34.800 --> 00:50:37.020
about the later second century BCE

00:50:37.020 --> 00:50:39.000
is how rapidly certain formal aspects

00:50:39.000 --> 00:50:40.920
of the writing of law consolidate,

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:42.540
alongside intellectual resources

00:50:42.540 --> 00:50:46.590
related to the predictable
use of written law.

00:50:46.590 --> 00:50:50.580
Savings clauses, that is, you know,

00:50:50.580 --> 00:50:52.147
places in a statute where you say,

00:50:52.147 --> 00:50:53.640
"Nothing in this statute is taken

00:50:53.640 --> 00:50:55.860
to contravene clause X
of this earlier statute,"

00:50:55.860 --> 00:50:59.520
which is most of the
occasions when laws are cited

00:50:59.520 --> 00:51:00.450
in the external legislation,

00:51:00.450 --> 00:51:02.730
they take of the form of savings clauses.

00:51:02.730 --> 00:51:05.730
Savings clauses are a
noteworthy case in point.

00:51:05.730 --> 00:51:08.010
Conventional tools of
statutory interpretation

00:51:08.010 --> 00:51:10.323
are obvious example of the second.

00:51:11.310 --> 00:51:16.310
Law, once written,
created its own reality.

00:51:16.620 --> 00:51:19.120
New histories of Roman law
should tell that story.

00:51:20.040 --> 00:51:22.690
It happens to be one for
which we have some evidence.

00:51:24.360 --> 00:51:25.877
Among the inventions
of the later tradition

00:51:25.877 --> 00:51:28.230
is the so-called lex Aebutia,

00:51:28.230 --> 00:51:29.340
the law that is credited

00:51:29.340 --> 00:51:31.390
with introducing the formulary procedure.

00:51:32.880 --> 00:51:36.030
Non-lawyers out there won't
care about the lex Aebutia,

00:51:36.030 --> 00:51:39.150
but you'll just indulge
me, please, for a moment.

00:51:39.150 --> 00:51:41.070
As I've stressed, the Romans did not know

00:51:41.070 --> 00:51:43.860
the identity of the person
who proposed this law,

00:51:43.860 --> 00:51:47.310
or the date, or a single
word of its language,

00:51:47.310 --> 00:51:49.260
despite, on their representations,

00:51:49.260 --> 00:51:52.533
its having affected a revolution
in legal and social life.

00:51:53.580 --> 00:51:55.203
This is simply not plausible.

00:51:56.040 --> 00:51:57.930
If the lex Aebutia existed,

00:51:57.930 --> 00:51:59.100
the most plausible reason

00:51:59.100 --> 00:52:00.930
for its light footprint in later memory

00:52:00.930 --> 00:52:02.283
is that it changed nothing.

00:52:03.390 --> 00:52:04.623
It was just forgettable.

00:52:06.870 --> 00:52:08.460
This brings me to my second topic,

00:52:08.460 --> 00:52:11.253
which is the science of
history, theirs and ours.

00:52:13.200 --> 00:52:15.960
One thing the lex Aebutia did not do

00:52:15.960 --> 00:52:18.120
is overturn some earlier world

00:52:18.120 --> 00:52:21.090
in which civil procedure
operated so rigidly

00:52:21.090 --> 00:52:23.430
that litigants who misspoke
a word of their script

00:52:23.430 --> 00:52:24.660
lost their case.

00:52:24.660 --> 00:52:27.573
I return now to that quotation
from Gaius about the trees.

00:52:29.100 --> 00:52:30.540
This model of archaic laws

00:52:30.540 --> 00:52:33.090
delivered to us principally by Gaius,

00:52:33.090 --> 00:52:34.503
but also by Jelius.

00:52:35.340 --> 00:52:36.990
Modern legal historians

00:52:36.990 --> 00:52:39.870
who are desperate for these
stupid anecdotes to be true

00:52:39.870 --> 00:52:42.120
never tire of praising Gaius

00:52:42.120 --> 00:52:44.700
as having had historical interests.

00:52:44.700 --> 00:52:47.400
And therefore, since he was a historian,

00:52:47.400 --> 00:52:48.600
he preserved for us,

00:52:48.600 --> 00:52:50.610
we can believe his evidence

00:52:50.610 --> 00:52:52.140
despite its being something like

00:52:52.140 --> 00:52:54.440
our only knowledge of
pre-formulary procedure.

00:52:56.490 --> 00:52:57.510
What reason do we think

00:52:57.510 --> 00:53:00.750
Gaius had reliable sources
for this information?

00:53:00.750 --> 00:53:01.980
None.

00:53:01.980 --> 00:53:06.060
On the contrary, his claims
about early Roman law

00:53:06.060 --> 00:53:08.490
sound exactly like other nonsense

00:53:08.490 --> 00:53:11.580
put forward by antiquarians
in the second century BCE

00:53:11.580 --> 00:53:15.480
as they speculated about how
different, how wildly crazy

00:53:15.480 --> 00:53:18.573
were the lives of archaic
versions of themselves.

00:53:20.580 --> 00:53:23.850
For example, to bring on some evidence

00:53:23.850 --> 00:53:25.400
from another moment in my life,

00:53:28.200 --> 00:53:31.440
we are told nearly the same
thing about the punctiliousness

00:53:31.440 --> 00:53:33.663
of Roman ritual in respect of religion.

00:53:34.920 --> 00:53:37.050
If a Roman got a gesture wrong,

00:53:37.050 --> 00:53:40.140
or misspoke a word, or
lacked an ingredient,

00:53:40.140 --> 00:53:42.570
or an errant noise was
made during the ritual,

00:53:42.570 --> 00:53:45.520
you had to restart the whole
damn thing from the beginning.

00:53:46.830 --> 00:53:49.530
The Romans even gave
this performance a name.

00:53:49.530 --> 00:53:51.543
It was called an instauratio.

00:53:53.220 --> 00:53:55.020
But this is a complete fabrication

00:53:55.020 --> 00:53:56.588
of the antiquarian tradition.

00:53:56.588 --> 00:54:00.030
It is virtually without
evidence from actual practice,

00:54:00.030 --> 00:54:05.030
and even from Roman, from
norms, from extant lege sacri.

00:54:07.320 --> 00:54:11.040
Among other things, in
these extant lege sacri,

00:54:11.040 --> 00:54:12.750
we have inscribed rules

00:54:12.750 --> 00:54:14.239
that regulate the substitution

00:54:14.239 --> 00:54:16.860
of one ingredient for another.

00:54:16.860 --> 00:54:18.180
So if you didn't have the ingredient,

00:54:18.180 --> 00:54:20.220
you just use something else.

00:54:20.220 --> 00:54:23.130
We know formulae, which
are again preserved,

00:54:23.130 --> 00:54:24.870
that you could utter by way of declaring

00:54:24.870 --> 00:54:26.670
that an error in performance

00:54:26.670 --> 00:54:28.590
was without ritual significance.

00:54:28.590 --> 00:54:30.097
If something went wrong, you just said,

00:54:30.097 --> 00:54:32.407
"Let it," you use the
third person imperative,

00:54:32.407 --> 00:54:34.230
"Let it be held to have
been performed correctly,"

00:54:34.230 --> 00:54:36.030
and you just went on with your life.

00:54:36.870 --> 00:54:37.703
And so on.

00:54:38.550 --> 00:54:40.590
Punctiliousness of this kind

00:54:40.590 --> 00:54:43.410
in which you then redid
the whole expensive ritual

00:54:43.410 --> 00:54:46.170
was something that one
attributed to one's ancestors

00:54:46.170 --> 00:54:49.593
by way of imagining an
earlier age of rectitude.

00:54:50.610 --> 00:54:52.920
But we decline to admit this

00:54:52.920 --> 00:54:54.960
because we have our own longing,

00:54:54.960 --> 00:54:57.240
in many ways, memetic of theirs,

00:54:57.240 --> 00:54:59.700
to know a world when men were men

00:54:59.700 --> 00:55:01.623
and Roman culture was pure.

00:55:04.260 --> 00:55:06.390
Another comparandum
exists in Pliny's history

00:55:06.390 --> 00:55:08.190
of Roman portraiture.

00:55:08.190 --> 00:55:09.330
Just by way of, I mean,

00:55:09.330 --> 00:55:10.620
you could really go on for a long time

00:55:10.620 --> 00:55:11.790
about silly things Romans say

00:55:11.790 --> 00:55:13.990
about earlier versions
of themselves, right?

00:55:15.150 --> 00:55:19.560
What I call Pliny's spinal tap
theory of Roman art, right?

00:55:19.560 --> 00:55:20.550
This is a joke about

00:55:20.550 --> 00:55:24.090
the little Stonehenge that
appears in spinal tap, right?

00:55:24.090 --> 00:55:25.440
'Cause according to Pliny,

00:55:25.440 --> 00:55:28.563
early Roman portrait statues
were about a meter tall.

00:55:30.210 --> 00:55:31.650
Now, it's not entirely clear

00:55:31.650 --> 00:55:35.160
how Pliny imagines these
statues to have functioned.

00:55:35.160 --> 00:55:36.720
Were early Romans very short,

00:55:36.720 --> 00:55:39.073
and these were just
naturalistic representations?

00:55:39.073 --> 00:55:40.080
(audience laughs)

00:55:40.080 --> 00:55:42.423
Were statue bases very tall?

00:55:43.980 --> 00:55:47.100
Instead of situating this
passage alongside others

00:55:47.100 --> 00:55:49.500
in which Antonine Romans speculate about

00:55:49.500 --> 00:55:53.820
the smallness or meanness
or so on of early crafts,

00:55:53.820 --> 00:55:56.160
and instead of just
bursting with laughter,

00:55:56.160 --> 00:55:59.010
one finds an amazing number of claims

00:55:59.010 --> 00:56:01.410
that Pliny probably found
this (speaking Latin),

00:56:02.490 --> 00:56:04.290
who might well have known people

00:56:04.290 --> 00:56:07.550
who reported on this statuary via autopsy,

00:56:07.550 --> 00:56:09.640
that said we've just generated this way

00:56:10.869 --> 00:56:13.110
of insisting that their
knowledge must be true

00:56:13.110 --> 00:56:15.760
because it's the kind of
thing we would like to know.

00:56:21.930 --> 00:56:23.970
Finally, let me say, as
regards the Roman state,

00:56:23.970 --> 00:56:25.380
so my final topic.

00:56:25.380 --> 00:56:28.710
As regards the Roman state
in the second century BCE,

00:56:28.710 --> 00:56:30.570
it's not simply that written documents

00:56:30.570 --> 00:56:33.990
start to survive in great
number after Hannibalic War.

00:56:33.990 --> 00:56:36.900
So do something like
contemporary literary accounts

00:56:36.900 --> 00:56:38.430
about the production of further documents

00:56:38.430 --> 00:56:39.573
that do not survive.

00:56:40.530 --> 00:56:44.340
As it happens, virtually
the same pattern holds

00:56:44.340 --> 00:56:46.293
for decrees of the Roman Senate.

00:56:47.670 --> 00:56:49.680
This is not accidental.

00:56:49.680 --> 00:56:51.300
And in that case too,

00:56:51.300 --> 00:56:53.630
I suggest that the pattern
of surviving evidence,

00:56:53.630 --> 00:56:55.350
of inscribed evidence,

00:56:55.350 --> 00:56:56.970
maps the history of the production

00:56:56.970 --> 00:56:58.830
of written senatorial decrees

00:56:58.830 --> 00:57:00.243
more or less precisely.

00:57:02.520 --> 00:57:04.590
This pattern could
issue from many factors,

00:57:04.590 --> 00:57:06.540
some of which are not mutually exclusive.

00:57:06.540 --> 00:57:08.070
That is to say, the pattern in our data

00:57:08.070 --> 00:57:10.680
is not likely to have a single cause.

00:57:10.680 --> 00:57:12.060
But two significant patterns

00:57:12.060 --> 00:57:15.960
in the content of this
material are worthy of remark.

00:57:15.960 --> 00:57:19.080
One, a great deal of the extant material

00:57:19.080 --> 00:57:21.060
arises through the recourse of foreigners

00:57:21.060 --> 00:57:21.960
to the Roman Senate

00:57:21.960 --> 00:57:23.973
as a forum for dispute resolution.

00:57:25.590 --> 00:57:27.227
This process clearly impelled

00:57:27.227 --> 00:57:30.090
the development of procedural
means to conduct hearings

00:57:30.090 --> 00:57:32.430
in what we might call
a rationalized fashion,

00:57:32.430 --> 00:57:34.560
as well as the development
of documentary means

00:57:34.560 --> 00:57:36.630
to represent those
hearings and their outcomes

00:57:36.630 --> 00:57:39.393
in a distinct social elect
and on distinct medium.

00:57:41.730 --> 00:57:43.140
This is, as it were, a corollary

00:57:43.140 --> 00:57:44.430
to a pattern I mentioned earlier,

00:57:44.430 --> 00:57:45.750
which is the one kind of document

00:57:45.750 --> 00:57:48.060
that the Romans may have written
before the Hannibalic War

00:57:48.060 --> 00:57:50.913
are treaties, because there
was a foreign counterparty.

00:57:52.830 --> 00:57:54.810
So it was the foreign counterparty

00:57:54.810 --> 00:57:56.880
that generated the need
to produce this thing

00:57:56.880 --> 00:57:57.963
in a permanent form.

00:57:59.640 --> 00:58:02.340
Second, the Senate is not suddenly visible

00:58:02.340 --> 00:58:03.660
in foreign affairs,

00:58:03.660 --> 00:58:06.120
which is to say, the
affairs between states.

00:58:06.120 --> 00:58:07.860
It is also suddenly visible

00:58:07.860 --> 00:58:10.233
guiding the conduct of magistrates.

00:58:11.070 --> 00:58:14.100
What is more, this pattern,

00:58:14.100 --> 00:58:15.570
the sudden interference of the Senate

00:58:15.570 --> 00:58:17.970
in what particular senators were doing,

00:58:17.970 --> 00:58:21.240
finds a correlate in data
provided by literary sources

00:58:21.240 --> 00:58:23.790
with a kind of claim to veracity

00:58:23.790 --> 00:58:26.220
regarding recourse of
members of the Roman elite

00:58:26.220 --> 00:58:29.250
to the people, namely, to
legislative assemblies,

00:58:29.250 --> 00:58:32.103
to trump the success of
rivals within the Senate.

00:58:34.290 --> 00:58:37.620
In other words, Senate and Comitium,

00:58:37.620 --> 00:58:39.780
to use a Fergus Miller-like phrase,

00:58:39.780 --> 00:58:43.530
senate and Comitium are
quite suddenly on record,

00:58:43.530 --> 00:58:45.510
frantically busy on record,

00:58:45.510 --> 00:58:47.640
directing the activities
of elected officials

00:58:47.640 --> 00:58:49.323
via legislative instruments.

00:58:50.550 --> 00:58:52.200
Although they appear in narrative

00:58:52.200 --> 00:58:55.020
as competing sources of authority

00:58:55.020 --> 00:58:58.470
adduced by rivals in any given contest,

00:58:58.470 --> 00:59:01.110
I think the activities
of Senate and Comitium

00:59:01.110 --> 00:59:04.413
should in fact be understood
as mutually constitutive.

00:59:05.460 --> 00:59:08.490
They rise to prominence as
competing sources of law

00:59:08.490 --> 00:59:11.700
because the nature of domestic
elite political competition

00:59:11.700 --> 00:59:13.770
was rapidly changing.

00:59:13.770 --> 00:59:14.820
In part, of course,

00:59:14.820 --> 00:59:17.823
in response to the changing
conditions of empire.

00:59:18.870 --> 00:59:21.000
It lay in the nature of elite competition

00:59:21.000 --> 00:59:24.120
that neither institution
was allowed a monopoly.

00:59:24.120 --> 00:59:26.640
The recourse of one person, or group,

00:59:26.640 --> 00:59:28.470
or faction to the Senate

00:59:28.470 --> 00:59:31.893
provoked their rivals to turn
to the people, and vice versa.

00:59:33.990 --> 00:59:37.230
But there is another level
in which competition,

00:59:37.230 --> 00:59:40.200
in this case, competition
between sources of law,

00:59:40.200 --> 00:59:43.383
both constitutes a space
and monopolizes it.

00:59:44.520 --> 00:59:46.868
The performance of contestation

00:59:46.868 --> 00:59:49.830
operates so as to convey the impression,

00:59:49.830 --> 00:59:52.440
by virtue of its noise and nature,

00:59:52.440 --> 00:59:55.080
that it exhausts and
covers the full terrain

00:59:55.080 --> 00:59:56.643
of plausible positions.

00:59:57.720 --> 01:00:00.660
The performance of competition
among sources of law

01:00:00.660 --> 01:00:02.610
thus work to exclude voices and issues

01:00:02.610 --> 01:00:05.283
not allowed into their staged cacophony.

01:00:06.780 --> 01:00:09.900
In short, when properly staged,

01:00:09.900 --> 01:00:11.400
competition among the elite

01:00:11.400 --> 01:00:14.640
assists in sustaining
the elite as a whole,

01:00:14.640 --> 01:00:15.603
as an elite.

01:00:16.980 --> 01:00:18.570
Turning to the Senate and Comitium,

01:00:18.570 --> 01:00:19.860
I suggested a moment ago

01:00:19.860 --> 01:00:21.330
that neither obtained a monopoly

01:00:21.330 --> 01:00:23.490
as a source of law in this period.

01:00:23.490 --> 01:00:24.990
Instead, you might say

01:00:24.990 --> 01:00:27.840
duopoly was the dominant form of monopoly

01:00:27.840 --> 01:00:29.163
in second century Rome.

01:00:31.050 --> 01:00:32.550
But a second order effect

01:00:32.550 --> 01:00:34.740
of conducting and
constraining elite competition

01:00:34.740 --> 01:00:36.480
through legislative enactment

01:00:36.480 --> 01:00:40.383
was an intense and sudden
deridification of politics.

01:00:43.170 --> 01:00:44.850
At Rome under the empire,

01:00:44.850 --> 01:00:46.890
there was a long period
in which the empire

01:00:46.890 --> 01:00:50.550
could be described as the
product of elite competition

01:00:50.550 --> 01:00:52.413
rather than an end in itself.

01:00:53.310 --> 01:00:54.900
In the language of Sallust,

01:00:54.900 --> 01:00:58.530
which was echoed by Augustine
and affirmed by Machiavelli,

01:00:58.530 --> 01:01:00.480
the virtue of the elite was expressed

01:01:00.480 --> 01:01:02.520
in a desire to dominate.

01:01:02.520 --> 01:01:05.340
And the project of empire
issued from a compromise

01:01:05.340 --> 01:01:06.930
whereby the desire to dominate

01:01:06.930 --> 01:01:09.030
was directed not at the masses at Rome,

01:01:09.030 --> 01:01:12.183
but outward to enemies
further and further afield.

01:01:13.560 --> 01:01:15.240
In consequence, a notable feature

01:01:15.240 --> 01:01:16.680
of Roman republican politics

01:01:16.680 --> 01:01:19.890
was that competition among the
elite for prestige and office

01:01:19.890 --> 01:01:22.833
took place, to a very large
degree, outside of Rome.

01:01:23.700 --> 01:01:25.830
It would not be wholly wrong to say that

01:01:25.830 --> 01:01:27.420
for a very long time,

01:01:27.420 --> 01:01:30.330
Roman foreign affairs
were an epiphenomenon

01:01:30.330 --> 01:01:31.863
of domestic politics.

01:01:34.290 --> 01:01:35.940
In the aftermath of the Third Punic War,

01:01:35.940 --> 01:01:38.220
and increasingly across
the second century BCE,

01:01:38.220 --> 01:01:40.020
the diversity and size of the empire

01:01:40.020 --> 01:01:43.200
affected an inversion in these priorities.

01:01:43.200 --> 01:01:44.580
It was Toynbee's argument

01:01:44.580 --> 01:01:47.580
that the Hannibalic War was
decisive in this transformation.

01:01:48.660 --> 01:01:50.850
The substance of any
given debate to one side,

01:01:50.850 --> 01:01:52.020
the effects of this are visible

01:01:52.020 --> 01:01:54.720
in the sudden subordination of priorities

01:01:54.720 --> 01:01:56.400
in the give and take of politics

01:01:56.400 --> 01:01:59.070
to the demand of collective constituencies

01:01:59.070 --> 01:02:02.040
for predictable institutional operations,

01:02:02.040 --> 01:02:03.840
in the light of vernacular understandings

01:02:03.840 --> 01:02:06.963
of what we might term second
order or public goods.

01:02:07.800 --> 01:02:09.330
Not only did magistrates have henceforth

01:02:09.330 --> 01:02:11.490
to be constrained in
their conduct vis-a-vis,

01:02:11.490 --> 01:02:14.010
conduct of politics vis-a-vis one another,

01:02:14.010 --> 01:02:15.720
but one could simply no longer assume

01:02:15.720 --> 01:02:17.970
a homology between the aggregated desires

01:02:17.970 --> 01:02:20.580
of individual members of
the elite for eminence

01:02:20.580 --> 01:02:22.950
and the larger needs of the state.

01:02:22.950 --> 01:02:25.563
Magistrates had to be controlled.

01:02:26.460 --> 01:02:28.740
Those controls are expressed
in the use of statute

01:02:28.740 --> 01:02:30.600
to instruct magistrates in office,

01:02:30.600 --> 01:02:33.270
and the passage of statutes
on magisterial malfeasance

01:02:33.270 --> 01:02:36.120
that gave outsiders
access to Roman tribunals

01:02:36.120 --> 01:02:37.830
at the very end of the century,

01:02:37.830 --> 01:02:39.720
in the placing of geographic constraints

01:02:39.720 --> 01:02:41.220
on magisterial action,

01:02:41.220 --> 01:02:43.290
which amounted to an incipient project

01:02:43.290 --> 01:02:46.083
of mapping and
territorializing the empire.

01:02:53.610 --> 01:02:54.900
So let me now just,

01:02:54.900 --> 01:02:58.500
let me conclude by
gesturing at three contexts

01:02:58.500 --> 01:03:01.353
by way of provoking some conversation,

01:03:02.310 --> 01:03:04.710
gesturing at contexts
that should be integrated

01:03:04.710 --> 01:03:07.140
with the story I've tried to tell.

01:03:07.140 --> 01:03:10.263
The first of this is historiography
of the First Punic War.

01:03:11.850 --> 01:03:12.930
I think it seems pretty clear

01:03:12.930 --> 01:03:14.310
that knowledge of the First Punic War

01:03:14.310 --> 01:03:15.900
was shaky and contestable already

01:03:15.900 --> 01:03:19.680
in the generation of Fabius Pictor.

01:03:19.680 --> 01:03:21.180
Frankly, I think it's pretty clear

01:03:21.180 --> 01:03:23.520
that the Romans themselves
knew nearly nothing about

01:03:23.520 --> 01:03:25.620
what happened in the First Punic War,

01:03:25.620 --> 01:03:26.820
and this would not be possible

01:03:26.820 --> 01:03:29.220
if the state were generating
loads of written documents

01:03:29.220 --> 01:03:30.053
in that era.

01:03:31.230 --> 01:03:33.990
That said, to return to
a phrase I used earlier,

01:03:33.990 --> 01:03:36.840
the performance of
contestation in historiography

01:03:36.840 --> 01:03:40.320
had similar effects to those
that I describe in politics.

01:03:40.320 --> 01:03:42.750
The fact that people
were disputing over facts

01:03:42.750 --> 01:03:44.400
made it appear that the disputants

01:03:44.400 --> 01:03:45.450
were arguing about something

01:03:45.450 --> 01:03:47.400
that was at least potentially knowable.

01:03:50.520 --> 01:03:53.700
Regarding the change from the
third century to the second,

01:03:53.700 --> 01:03:56.850
I observed as well that
whatever the, sorry,

01:03:56.850 --> 01:04:00.270
I want to observe that whatever
the Roman government did

01:04:00.270 --> 01:04:01.290
in the earlier period,

01:04:01.290 --> 01:04:03.840
whatever the Roman government
did in the third century,

01:04:03.840 --> 01:04:06.720
it revisited those processes
later in the second

01:04:06.720 --> 01:04:10.383
when, on my claim, the Romans
started to use writing.

01:04:14.040 --> 01:04:15.750
Well, this is, well, I'll tell it.

01:04:15.750 --> 01:04:16.657
How, this is not the,

01:04:16.657 --> 01:04:18.930
actually, I won't go
back to the blank slide.

01:04:18.930 --> 01:04:21.540
But how else are we to explain the fact

01:04:21.540 --> 01:04:25.140
that the Romans wrote a law
on jurisdiction for Sicily

01:04:25.140 --> 01:04:27.660
only in the year 132,

01:04:27.660 --> 01:04:31.200
109 years after they
made Sicily a province?

01:04:31.200 --> 01:04:33.630
Or in this case, right?

01:04:33.630 --> 01:04:34.463
How are we,

01:04:36.000 --> 01:04:39.150
this is an inscription from Aquileia

01:04:39.150 --> 01:04:41.433
on the occasion of a
supplementary deduction,

01:04:42.720 --> 01:04:45.360
the settling there of a
supplementary group of colonists

01:04:45.360 --> 01:04:46.807
in the year 169.

01:04:46.807 --> 01:04:49.980
"Titus Annius Luscus, son
of Titus, the triumvir.

01:04:49.980 --> 01:04:51.870
He provided for the
construction of this temple

01:04:51.870 --> 01:04:52.703
and dedicated it.

01:04:52.703 --> 01:04:53.820
He composed and delivered laws,

01:04:53.820 --> 01:04:55.970
and three times he
enrolled in the Senate."

01:04:58.650 --> 01:05:01.320
So why is it only in 169 BC

01:05:01.320 --> 01:05:03.600
on the occasion of a
supplementary deduction

01:05:03.600 --> 01:05:05.553
that Aquileia received laws?

01:05:07.110 --> 01:05:09.180
And of course, there's the
very interesting problem

01:05:09.180 --> 01:05:10.380
that on its own account,

01:05:10.380 --> 01:05:12.930
Rome only began to write poetry in 240

01:05:12.930 --> 01:05:14.820
and history a generation later.

01:05:14.820 --> 01:05:18.150
And each of these early
endeavors was in Greek.

01:05:18.150 --> 01:05:19.770
Rome's first taking up in writing,

01:05:19.770 --> 01:05:23.310
which is to say its late, late,
late taking up of writing,

01:05:23.310 --> 01:05:25.470
was nevertheless felt
so frontal an assault

01:05:25.470 --> 01:05:27.090
on aristocratic prestige

01:05:27.090 --> 01:05:29.460
that Roman literature
had initially to deny

01:05:29.460 --> 01:05:30.663
its own existence.

01:05:31.830 --> 01:05:33.127
Thank you very much.

01:05:33.127 --> 01:05:36.127
(audience applauds)

01:05:46.456 --> 01:05:48.780
