01:08:05.940 --> 01:08:10.940
So, I mean, I suppose I kind of believe

01:08:13.080 --> 01:08:14.910
they have to be connected, right?

01:08:14.910 --> 01:08:17.310
And it has something to do with,

01:08:17.310 --> 01:08:20.340
it must have something to
do with, as you term it,

01:08:20.340 --> 01:08:22.190
something like aristocratic prestige.

01:08:25.980 --> 01:08:27.960
What I will say,

01:08:27.960 --> 01:08:29.040
I could try to give a longer answer,

01:08:29.040 --> 01:08:30.780
but let me just gesture to kind of

01:08:30.780 --> 01:08:32.850
a way of thinking about the problem,

01:08:32.850 --> 01:08:37.850
that there is a kind of
literature out there in the world,

01:08:38.730 --> 01:08:40.950
a fun literature, a diverse literature,

01:08:40.950 --> 01:08:44.130
'cause it comes from many
disciplinary viewpoints,

01:08:44.130 --> 01:08:45.460
on the origins of writing

01:08:46.740 --> 01:08:47.820
and the connection between writing

01:08:47.820 --> 01:08:50.220
and certain kinds of
institutions of social authority

01:08:50.220 --> 01:08:51.540
or between governmental knowledge.

01:08:51.540 --> 01:08:55.080
So everything from Jack
Goody to Wang Haicheng.

01:08:55.080 --> 01:08:56.730
Really great stuff.

01:08:56.730 --> 01:08:59.130
I know of no comparable literature,

01:08:59.130 --> 01:09:01.690
I know of no comparable
comparative literature

01:09:03.420 --> 01:09:07.800
on a number of populations,
culture, civilizations,

01:09:07.800 --> 01:09:08.670
whatever term you want to use,

01:09:08.670 --> 01:09:11.730
that we can name that deliberately
did not take up writing,

01:09:11.730 --> 01:09:13.620
that were surrounded by populations

01:09:13.620 --> 01:09:15.213
that used writing and did not.

01:09:16.290 --> 01:09:19.083
So one could think about the,

01:09:20.520 --> 01:09:22.950
one could think about the, you know,

01:09:22.950 --> 01:09:26.310
the early Persian tradition, right?

01:09:26.310 --> 01:09:28.770
And the declining, in particular,

01:09:28.770 --> 01:09:30.600
to write down the "Avesta."

01:09:30.600 --> 01:09:34.170
But the simple non-use of writing

01:09:34.170 --> 01:09:36.180
for recording in Persian, in particular,

01:09:36.180 --> 01:09:39.330
until this extraordinarily
late moment, right?

01:09:39.330 --> 01:09:42.450
You can think about the
very late introduction

01:09:42.450 --> 01:09:45.690
of technologies of literacy
into the Sanskrit tradition,

01:09:45.690 --> 01:09:46.890
which is very old,

01:09:46.890 --> 01:09:48.690
but not nearly as old,
so far as one can tell,

01:09:48.690 --> 01:09:50.340
as the Sanskrit tradition itself.

01:09:51.240 --> 01:09:53.400
And you could say Rome.

01:09:53.400 --> 01:09:55.860
And so there are at least
three cases that come to mind,

01:09:55.860 --> 01:09:57.600
but there must be others out there

01:09:57.600 --> 01:09:59.640
in which these cultures exist

01:09:59.640 --> 01:10:01.860
surrounded by other
people who use writing,

01:10:01.860 --> 01:10:03.410
and they insist on not writing.

01:10:04.260 --> 01:10:09.260
And, you know, the
co-occurrence, you said,

01:10:10.800 --> 01:10:14.310
you know, of the use of the
technologies of literacy

01:10:14.310 --> 01:10:15.420
for governmental knowledge,

01:10:15.420 --> 01:10:17.580
and then for these other forms of,

01:10:17.580 --> 01:10:19.080
I don't know, cultural production,

01:10:19.080 --> 01:10:20.820
for lack of a better word.

01:10:20.820 --> 01:10:22.290
There must be,

01:10:22.290 --> 01:10:24.540
there must be at least one
story to tell about them

01:10:24.540 --> 01:10:26.090
that brings all those together.

01:10:27.330 --> 01:10:28.650
I said that at the end
in the hopes, really,

01:10:28.650 --> 01:10:30.840
that somebody else would
do that writing for me.

01:10:30.840 --> 01:10:32.240
I don't have a great answer.

01:10:33.090 --> 01:10:33.923
Thank you.

01:10:36.720 --> 01:10:37.553
Sure.

01:10:43.050 --> 01:10:43.883
- [Audience Member] Hello.

01:10:43.883 --> 01:10:47.340
Thank you so much, this
is really so fascinating.

01:10:47.340 --> 01:10:50.610
My question is about how we slot,

01:10:50.610 --> 01:10:53.100
or how you see Roman law operating

01:10:53.100 --> 01:10:56.400
before you see writing coming into it.

01:10:56.400 --> 01:10:59.730
And sort of related to that,

01:10:59.730 --> 01:11:02.190
whether one of the reasons why

01:11:02.190 --> 01:11:05.220
we don't see anything really
cited before the Hannibalic War

01:11:05.220 --> 01:11:07.710
is something to do with
what happens in that war

01:11:07.710 --> 01:11:10.380
and the sort of massive loss of life

01:11:10.380 --> 01:11:14.040
that our sources suggest
happened through that,

01:11:14.040 --> 01:11:15.210
and whether there was some sort of

01:11:15.210 --> 01:11:16.890
loss of knowledge, perhaps,

01:11:16.890 --> 01:11:19.443
and of that oral tradition.

01:11:32.760 --> 01:11:33.930
- So I can say something about

01:11:33.930 --> 01:11:36.390
the first part of your question.

01:11:36.390 --> 01:11:39.780
How did Roman law function
before the Hannibalic War?

01:11:39.780 --> 01:11:42.690
And it would just be a total guess,

01:11:42.690 --> 01:11:44.730
be my guess as opposed
to someone else's guess.

01:11:44.730 --> 01:11:49.730
And I suppose I could riff
on this kind of question of,

01:11:50.227 --> 01:11:54.390
you know, like trauma, violence,
destruction of property,

01:11:54.390 --> 01:11:56.043
blah, blah, blah, you know.

01:11:57.270 --> 01:11:59.070
Although I will say that, I mean,

01:11:59.070 --> 01:12:00.120
to make a joke about other people

01:12:00.120 --> 01:12:01.200
who write about this sort of stuff,

01:12:01.200 --> 01:12:03.660
i.e., obviously Walter Scheidel,

01:12:03.660 --> 01:12:05.010
what we don't see in Rome

01:12:05.010 --> 01:12:07.410
is a massive destruction of property

01:12:07.410 --> 01:12:09.360
which then issues in a 25 year period

01:12:09.360 --> 01:12:10.620
after the Hannibalic War,

01:12:10.620 --> 01:12:11.880
when all of a sudden every, you know,

01:12:11.880 --> 01:12:13.230
like property regimes in Rome

01:12:13.230 --> 01:12:15.990
are just vastly more
egalitarian because of,

01:12:15.990 --> 01:12:19.080
you know, it's like the elite
stranglehold on ownership

01:12:19.080 --> 01:12:19.913
was somehow broken,

01:12:19.913 --> 01:12:22.920
so the Hannibalic War,

01:12:22.920 --> 01:12:24.810
there are, to be more serious,

01:12:24.810 --> 01:12:27.660
there are areas of Roman
life after the Hannibalic War

01:12:27.660 --> 01:12:30.360
which do not seem to reflect
massive social trauma.

01:12:30.360 --> 01:12:32.243
I mean, there are obviously areas that do.

01:12:33.750 --> 01:12:38.750
I assume that Roman law
before the formulary system

01:12:39.330 --> 01:12:43.170
used something like a formulary system.

01:12:43.170 --> 01:12:46.690
That is to say, and by
that, what I mean is that

01:12:48.360 --> 01:12:50.670
what happens in a formulary system,

01:12:50.670 --> 01:12:52.170
there is ways you could modify this,

01:12:52.170 --> 01:12:55.260
but what happens in the
formulary system is,

01:12:55.260 --> 01:12:57.060
you know, the two litigants

01:12:57.060 --> 01:13:01.500
show up in front of the
person in authority,

01:13:01.500 --> 01:13:04.443
in the later case, in the
classical system, a magistrate,

01:13:05.370 --> 01:13:07.623
who has the right to speak the law,

01:13:08.550 --> 01:13:10.320
who's allowed to (speaking Latin),

01:13:10.320 --> 01:13:13.410
and they tell their story, right?

01:13:13.410 --> 01:13:18.410
And they say all sorts of
crazy bullshit, you know,

01:13:18.540 --> 01:13:21.690
about, you know, "My neighbor's
trees fell into my yard."

01:13:21.690 --> 01:13:22.837
And then they throw another
thing like, you know,

01:13:22.837 --> 01:13:24.690
"He's crazy, he also
does it with his trash.

01:13:24.690 --> 01:13:26.280
His dog barks all night, I can't sleep."

01:13:26.280 --> 01:13:27.210
They throw out all sorts of stuff

01:13:27.210 --> 01:13:29.670
as people do when they
narrate their own problems.

01:13:29.670 --> 01:13:34.670
And when the magistrate produces a formula

01:13:36.570 --> 01:13:37.830
in classical Roman law,

01:13:37.830 --> 01:13:40.320
he produces a decision tree

01:13:40.320 --> 01:13:42.360
in which he narrows
down the entire dispute

01:13:42.360 --> 01:13:45.187
to a very complicated, long
sentence in which he says,

01:13:45.187 --> 01:13:46.707
"If this, then why?"

01:13:47.550 --> 01:13:50.010
And then there can be a
cascading set of decisions,

01:13:50.010 --> 01:13:52.770
like if this, but if there's
an exception, go here, and on.

01:13:52.770 --> 01:13:54.090
It looks like a tree, right?

01:13:54.090 --> 01:13:58.230
And in which both a law,

01:13:58.230 --> 01:14:00.690
a norm is brought to bear on the problem

01:14:00.690 --> 01:14:02.730
and the case is re-narrated

01:14:02.730 --> 01:14:05.580
so that only the salient
facts are mentioned.

01:14:05.580 --> 01:14:08.970
And the job of the judge is
to decide which the facts go

01:14:08.970 --> 01:14:10.350
toggle A, toggle B.

01:14:10.350 --> 01:14:13.950
And I sort of assume that
instead of this silly business

01:14:13.950 --> 01:14:16.650
in which there was only
five actions in Roman law,

01:14:16.650 --> 01:14:18.780
and you had, you know, no matter
what your dispute was over,

01:14:18.780 --> 01:14:20.400
you had to say trees,

01:14:20.400 --> 01:14:22.230
then in fact, much of what happened before

01:14:22.230 --> 01:14:24.390
is exactly what happens
in the formulary system.

01:14:24.390 --> 01:14:27.183
You came up, the two litigants
described their problem,

01:14:28.230 --> 01:14:29.063
and somebody,

01:14:29.063 --> 01:14:31.650
and you know, what might've
been different, frankly,

01:14:31.650 --> 01:14:33.390
is the existence of lay judges.

01:14:33.390 --> 01:14:35.420
Or it could be that
before the lex Aebutia,

01:14:35.420 --> 01:14:37.470
it was all handled by pontiffs,

01:14:37.470 --> 01:14:38.880
and later it was handled by magistrates,

01:14:38.880 --> 01:14:40.560
except that we know
that there were praetors

01:14:40.560 --> 01:14:43.530
who handled jurisdiction
before the lex Aebutia.

01:14:43.530 --> 01:14:45.240
So I just, I mean,

01:14:45.240 --> 01:14:47.490
and it's for the reason that we think

01:14:47.490 --> 01:14:50.190
we have knowledge of praetors
with jurisdiction before

01:14:50.190 --> 01:14:54.477
that I suspect that law pre-lex Aebutia

01:14:57.630 --> 01:15:00.510
probably looked something
like the formulary system.

01:15:00.510 --> 01:15:02.880
I mean, despite the,

01:15:02.880 --> 01:15:05.550
historians of Roman law loved to insist

01:15:05.550 --> 01:15:07.470
in a kind of really profound way

01:15:07.470 --> 01:15:12.470
on the difference between
the cognitio procedure

01:15:12.570 --> 01:15:13.980
and formulary procedure.

01:15:13.980 --> 01:15:15.000
And then if you actually look at

01:15:15.000 --> 01:15:17.600
the actual conduct of cognitio procedure,

01:15:17.600 --> 01:15:20.130
it looks an awful lot
like formulary procedure.

01:15:20.130 --> 01:15:22.170
I mean, why the Romans were insistent

01:15:22.170 --> 01:15:23.190
is an ideological matter.

01:15:23.190 --> 01:15:28.190
But that, again, I think
that something like

01:15:29.926 --> 01:15:32.640
the technologies of procedures
of elitum, the very few,

01:15:32.640 --> 01:15:35.010
and some of them, I mean, now
I'm gonna say one last thing,

01:15:35.010 --> 01:15:36.510
and then I swear I'll shut up.

01:15:38.490 --> 01:15:40.860
Some of the aspects of
this procedure quite,

01:15:40.860 --> 01:15:42.210
I'm gonna step aside from the whole issue

01:15:42.210 --> 01:15:45.120
of the stupid pontiffs and
ritual punctiliousness.

01:15:45.120 --> 01:15:46.620
A lot of aspects of their procedure

01:15:46.620 --> 01:15:49.450
are probably overdetermined
by the fact that

01:15:51.600 --> 01:15:53.220
there were no mechanisms of state power

01:15:53.220 --> 01:15:54.090
that could compel somebody

01:15:54.090 --> 01:15:57.093
to participate in civil litigation, right?

01:15:59.070 --> 01:15:59.903
Right?

01:15:59.903 --> 01:16:01.020
So if you wanted to sue your neighbor

01:16:01.020 --> 01:16:03.990
and he refused to show up,
you're basically screwed.

01:16:03.990 --> 01:16:07.050
And the Romans developed
mechanisms legally,

01:16:07.050 --> 01:16:07.890
but not instrumentally.

01:16:07.890 --> 01:16:08.970
There was never a police officer.

01:16:08.970 --> 01:16:10.230
But there were legal means

01:16:10.230 --> 01:16:12.720
you could bring to bear eventually

01:16:12.720 --> 01:16:15.510
to force your neighbor to
actually show up in court.

01:16:15.510 --> 01:16:17.100
But for a long time, these
things did not exist.

01:16:17.100 --> 01:16:21.150
The Romans clearly date the
introduction of these things

01:16:21.150 --> 01:16:22.560
extremely late.

01:16:22.560 --> 01:16:26.347
So for a long time, you had the,

01:16:26.347 --> 01:16:27.690
if a plaintiff filed a lawsuit,

01:16:27.690 --> 01:16:30.450
the defendant had to be
persuaded to show up.

01:16:30.450 --> 01:16:32.790
And there is a plausible case to be made

01:16:32.790 --> 01:16:34.470
that one of the things that,

01:16:34.470 --> 01:16:36.060
one of the reasons why Roman procedure

01:16:36.060 --> 01:16:37.560
looks the way it does

01:16:37.560 --> 01:16:39.600
is because you had to give the defendant,

01:16:39.600 --> 01:16:42.510
you had to give the
defendant enough confidence

01:16:42.510 --> 01:16:45.090
that he'd be willing to show up at all.

01:16:45.090 --> 01:16:46.380
I could go on,

01:16:46.380 --> 01:16:49.290
but I think that aspect is
probably much more interesting,

01:16:49.290 --> 01:16:52.020
and probably also that
that kind of interpretation

01:16:52.020 --> 01:16:53.250
should be brought to bear,

01:16:53.250 --> 01:16:54.450
because it's a kind of real condition

01:16:54.450 --> 01:16:55.560
that we know something about,

01:16:55.560 --> 01:16:58.160
rather than this kind of
like mythological nonsense.

01:17:07.620 --> 01:17:08.453
- [Audience Member] Thank you so much

01:17:08.453 --> 01:17:09.780
for the fascinating talk.

01:17:09.780 --> 01:17:14.780
And I'm very fascinated
by this concept of like

01:17:15.060 --> 01:17:16.950
myth histories that you talked about,

01:17:16.950 --> 01:17:19.770
and this kind of legal history

01:17:19.770 --> 01:17:21.870
in constructing a Roman identity,

01:17:21.870 --> 01:17:26.580
especially in relation
to outside foreign forces

01:17:26.580 --> 01:17:29.520
that they're now coming into contact with

01:17:29.520 --> 01:17:32.190
in the second century BCE.

01:17:32.190 --> 01:17:35.250
And I was wondering if
you could talk more about

01:17:35.250 --> 01:17:38.730
who specifically do you think
the Romans are talking to

01:17:38.730 --> 01:17:40.410
when developing these laws?

01:17:40.410 --> 01:17:44.790
And also what kind of
dialogues and messages

01:17:44.790 --> 01:17:47.760
do you think that they
are trying to send out

01:17:47.760 --> 01:17:50.013
to, like, these other foreign,

01:17:51.120 --> 01:17:52.770
these foreign nations?

01:17:52.770 --> 01:17:53.603
- Okay.

01:18:01.260 --> 01:18:06.260
It's about legislation,

01:18:06.570 --> 01:18:07.710
somewhat harder, but I will say,

01:18:07.710 --> 01:18:09.870
let me just say a word, digress to,

01:18:09.870 --> 01:18:11.850
step aside to the other
body of evidence I cited

01:18:11.850 --> 01:18:13.740
that one could take up,

01:18:13.740 --> 01:18:15.663
which is decrees of the Senate.

01:18:18.630 --> 01:18:20.100
Actually, the decrees of the Senate

01:18:20.100 --> 01:18:22.440
are an interesting thing to cite

01:18:22.440 --> 01:18:24.573
in respect of legal procedure, right?

01:18:28.026 --> 01:18:28.860
If you look at decree,

01:18:28.860 --> 01:18:31.810
the early decrees of
the Senate that we have

01:18:32.820 --> 01:18:36.570
almost always concern
something like foreign affairs.

01:18:36.570 --> 01:18:37.440
It so happens that, of course,

01:18:37.440 --> 01:18:42.150
many of them are epigraphically
preserved in Greek

01:18:42.150 --> 01:18:43.083
in the east.

01:18:44.100 --> 01:18:48.060
And interestingly, when
I said something like,

01:18:48.060 --> 01:18:48.990
they developed this kind of

01:18:48.990 --> 01:18:53.283
rationalized means for
representing adversarial speech,

01:18:55.170 --> 01:18:56.003
they, you know,

01:18:56.003 --> 01:18:57.900
you could imagine them
looking like two litigants

01:18:57.900 --> 01:18:58.980
coming from the court, right?

01:18:58.980 --> 01:19:03.210
So a senatus consultum very
rapidly develops this mechanism

01:19:03.210 --> 01:19:04.800
for saying, you know,

01:19:04.800 --> 01:19:06.330
these people came before us,

01:19:06.330 --> 01:19:08.635
so-and-so, you know,

01:19:08.635 --> 01:19:10.920
concerning the matter at hand said this,

01:19:10.920 --> 01:19:13.110
so-and-so concerning the
matter at hand said that,

01:19:13.110 --> 01:19:13.943
concerning the matter

01:19:13.943 --> 01:19:15.810
which was immediately prior discussed,

01:19:15.810 --> 01:19:16.650
blah, blah, and it goes on.

01:19:16.650 --> 01:19:18.570
And there's all these markers.

01:19:18.570 --> 01:19:21.390
And the speech of the
litigants is always paraphrased

01:19:21.390 --> 01:19:23.220
in a way that makes them

01:19:23.220 --> 01:19:25.953
appear to have spoken remarkably on point.

01:19:26.910 --> 01:19:29.400
But that's also rationalized
in the sense that,

01:19:29.400 --> 01:19:32.670
so that again, all the
chaff about, you know,

01:19:32.670 --> 01:19:35.070
all the things that you might
have imagined that a Greek,

01:19:35.070 --> 01:19:36.270
I'm supposed to stand in
front of the microphone.

01:19:36.270 --> 01:19:37.530
All the chaff,

01:19:37.530 --> 01:19:39.810
all the stuff that you can
imagine a Greek orator saying,

01:19:39.810 --> 01:19:42.180
like, "Oh, it's so wonderful

01:19:42.180 --> 01:19:44.580
to be before this most
distinguished body,"

01:19:44.580 --> 01:19:46.680
and you know, blah, blah, blah.

01:19:46.680 --> 01:19:48.330
And you know, "I come here

01:19:48.330 --> 01:19:51.360
mindful of the extraordinarily
long history of relations

01:19:51.360 --> 01:19:53.100
between your city and mine,

01:19:53.100 --> 01:19:54.877
and the fact that," you
know, blah, blah, blah,

01:19:54.877 --> 01:19:57.420
"we both worship Aphrodite,"
and all this other crap.

01:19:57.420 --> 01:19:59.820
None of that is in the represented speech,

01:19:59.820 --> 01:20:02.370
paraphrased speech in
the senatus consulta.

01:20:02.370 --> 01:20:03.660
If you look at a senatus consulta,

01:20:03.660 --> 01:20:04.627
somebody came and said,

01:20:04.627 --> 01:20:06.997
"Please allow me to cite the
facts relevant to," you know,

01:20:06.997 --> 01:20:08.760
"my claim in respect of the
ownership of (speaking Latin)

01:20:08.760 --> 01:20:11.393
And on, on, on, on, on, on, on, on.

01:20:14.670 --> 01:20:17.740
So they, you can see there something like

01:20:19.110 --> 01:20:21.840
the technology of memory that might have,

01:20:21.840 --> 01:20:23.700
a technology of memory that looks,

01:20:23.700 --> 01:20:26.640
must be homeomorphic with the kind of,

01:20:26.640 --> 01:20:27.870
with what a magistrate was doing

01:20:27.870 --> 01:20:30.093
when people brought speech before a court.

01:20:33.870 --> 01:20:38.870
So the senatus consultum
develops in a way,

01:20:40.500 --> 01:20:42.600
in a kind of interesting way, right?

01:20:42.600 --> 01:20:47.600
So that where the technology
of the letter, right,

01:20:50.730 --> 01:20:51.563
that goes back from

01:20:51.563 --> 01:20:54.120
the magistrate sends back to the thing

01:20:54.120 --> 01:20:56.610
essentially repeats in a kind of

01:20:56.610 --> 01:20:59.220
very different form of summary.

01:20:59.220 --> 01:21:01.290
The content of whatever thing, you know,

01:21:01.290 --> 01:21:04.380
the ambassador from Chicago
brought to New Haven.

01:21:04.380 --> 01:21:05.880
The senatus consultum,

01:21:05.880 --> 01:21:07.860
which has to render a
decision on the matter,

01:21:07.860 --> 01:21:12.600
gives an extremely different
summary, which isn't,

01:21:12.600 --> 01:21:15.213
and it is, I think, the,

01:21:16.740 --> 01:21:19.290
it is, I think, the arrival of foreigners

01:21:19.290 --> 01:21:22.500
that impelled the creation
of this technology.

01:21:22.500 --> 01:21:24.900
I mean, there is no particular reason

01:21:24.900 --> 01:21:27.300
to think that before that moment,

01:21:27.300 --> 01:21:30.270
the Romans had a particular
set of mechanisms

01:21:30.270 --> 01:21:34.650
for representing adversarial
speech in a written document.

01:21:34.650 --> 01:21:37.050
So that would be one answer.

01:21:37.050 --> 01:21:40.203
You know, about legislation,

01:21:41.280 --> 01:21:43.140
I think it's much harder to tell.

01:21:43.140 --> 01:21:46.470
It is noteworthy that the story
the Romans themselves tell

01:21:46.470 --> 01:21:47.580
about writing legislation

01:21:47.580 --> 01:21:49.290
is that they wandered
out to look for models

01:21:49.290 --> 01:21:50.853
about how to write it up.

01:21:52.590 --> 01:21:54.210
And that may be itself a version

01:21:54.210 --> 01:21:56.410
of one of these kinds
of doublets I suggest.

01:21:57.660 --> 01:21:58.713
I should add,

01:21:59.580 --> 01:22:01.230
I know why this thought
occurred to me just now,

01:22:01.230 --> 01:22:06.230
that, you know, the kind
of picture I'm presenting,

01:22:07.110 --> 01:22:11.100
which is a picture of
one kind about, you know,

01:22:11.100 --> 01:22:13.080
what I take to have been a
fairly massive set of changes

01:22:13.080 --> 01:22:14.490
that I think probably did take place

01:22:14.490 --> 01:22:16.020
at the start of the second century

01:22:16.020 --> 01:22:17.880
in consequence of the Hannibalic War.

01:22:17.880 --> 01:22:19.750
You know, the Romans did tell a story

01:22:20.700 --> 01:22:22.680
about certain kinds of social revolutions

01:22:22.680 --> 01:22:25.710
that took place as a
result of the opening up,

01:22:25.710 --> 01:22:28.887
especially of wars in the east, you know.

01:22:28.887 --> 01:22:30.660
And I wouldn't discount that.

01:22:30.660 --> 01:22:33.630
I'm sure that in a
variety of different ways,

01:22:33.630 --> 01:22:35.820
both as something like their relationship

01:22:35.820 --> 01:22:40.820
to a new form of encounter
with cultures of writing,

01:22:41.430 --> 01:22:44.520
but also in respect of
something like political economy

01:22:44.520 --> 01:22:46.260
as a result of the importation of wealth,

01:22:46.260 --> 01:22:47.760
that some of their historiography

01:22:47.760 --> 01:22:50.610
about change in the second
century is surely accurate.

01:22:50.610 --> 01:22:54.180
Not everything the Romans say
about their own past is dumb.

01:22:54.180 --> 01:22:55.830
That would be a takeaway point there.

01:22:55.830 --> 01:22:58.020
That's not a great
answer for your question.

01:22:58.020 --> 01:22:59.790
I hadn't thought about that aspect of it.

01:22:59.790 --> 01:23:02.123
- [Noel] One more question,
(speaking faintly).

01:23:02.123 --> 01:23:03.489
One more question, anybody?

01:23:03.489 --> 01:23:06.390
- [Audience Member] Yeah.

01:23:06.390 --> 01:23:07.860
Actually, this might be a good follow on

01:23:07.860 --> 01:23:09.540
to just your last point just there.

01:23:09.540 --> 01:23:10.470
And I'm wondering sort of

01:23:10.470 --> 01:23:14.673
outside of the realm of civil
procedure and statute law,

01:23:15.900 --> 01:23:18.600
what we can discern, or you
think we can be discerned

01:23:18.600 --> 01:23:21.540
just about sort of constitutional order

01:23:21.540 --> 01:23:22.703
in the middle Republic, early Republic,

01:23:22.703 --> 01:23:25.260
I mean, and whether this sort of,

01:23:25.260 --> 01:23:26.520
which is often unwritten, anyway.

01:23:26.520 --> 01:23:29.430
I mean, very little of the
fragments of the Twelve Tables

01:23:29.430 --> 01:23:31.110
even have to do with kind
of constitutional law.

01:23:31.110 --> 01:23:33.180
But from some of the historiography,

01:23:33.180 --> 01:23:34.890
maybe from Pictor, whom you
mentioned, for instance,

01:23:34.890 --> 01:23:38.460
we get a sense of, you
know, consul offices,

01:23:38.460 --> 01:23:41.463
constitutional offices and et cetera.

01:23:42.420 --> 01:23:44.880
So yeah, so I'm wondering if that,

01:23:44.880 --> 01:23:46.950
the constitutional law, you think,

01:23:46.950 --> 01:23:49.740
what Romans had to say about
their own constitutional order

01:23:49.740 --> 01:23:51.790
might be something about which we have

01:23:52.980 --> 01:23:54.870
some more ability to discern

01:23:54.870 --> 01:23:58.440
outside of the realms of
civil procedure and statute.

01:23:58.440 --> 01:24:00.263
But just curious, your comments on that?

01:24:02.310 --> 01:24:03.143
- I'll make.

01:24:04.710 --> 01:24:05.700
I was about to make some promise,

01:24:05.700 --> 01:24:07.110
like I'll make two quick replies.

01:24:07.110 --> 01:24:09.350
I'm not very good at that, but I'll, so...

01:24:14.280 --> 01:24:18.210
Two things that seem to be
true about the Romans before.

01:24:18.210 --> 01:24:22.650
So there is one area of public law where

01:24:22.650 --> 01:24:24.270
I think it was Peter Brunt insisted

01:24:24.270 --> 01:24:26.700
the Romans might have had real knowledge.

01:24:26.700 --> 01:24:28.628
In a moment when Peter Brunt,

01:24:28.628 --> 01:24:29.640
I can't find the passage,

01:24:29.640 --> 01:24:31.290
but I think Brunt said somewhere

01:24:31.290 --> 01:24:32.520
that he doesn't believe the Romans

01:24:32.520 --> 01:24:34.140
actually knew anything
about their own past,

01:24:34.140 --> 01:24:39.140
except possibly, he offers, I
think, in the same paragraph,

01:24:40.020 --> 01:24:41.160
two possibilities.

01:24:41.160 --> 01:24:43.230
One, that they remembered something about

01:24:43.230 --> 01:24:45.000
big grants of citizenship,

01:24:45.000 --> 01:24:46.500
because it was kinda like a thing

01:24:46.500 --> 01:24:48.990
for the state to all of a
sudden admit new people.

01:24:48.990 --> 01:24:50.490
I actually don't believe that.

01:24:51.510 --> 01:24:56.403
But that the history of the
expansion of the tribal system,

01:24:58.770 --> 01:25:01.413
that they may have had
some sense, right, of,

01:25:05.709 --> 01:25:07.620
because of the materiality
of the extension

01:25:07.620 --> 01:25:10.920
of the tribal system,
it did actually, right,

01:25:10.920 --> 01:25:13.080
they may have, it was easy to guess

01:25:13.080 --> 01:25:14.460
in what order the tribes were added.

01:25:14.460 --> 01:25:15.990
Even if you didn't have
a narrative to tell you,

01:25:15.990 --> 01:25:17.580
it would be easy to guess in what sort of,

01:25:17.580 --> 01:25:19.770
make a guess what order
the tribes were added

01:25:19.770 --> 01:25:22.140
and blah, blah, so that's one thing.

01:25:22.140 --> 01:25:22.973
I should mention that

01:25:22.973 --> 01:25:25.140
in the wider context of
what we're talking about.

01:25:25.140 --> 01:25:26.853
Then there's about public law.

01:25:28.770 --> 01:25:30.240
I mean, two things that do seem to be true

01:25:30.240 --> 01:25:32.160
about Roman public law,

01:25:32.160 --> 01:25:34.140
which you can guess on the basis of things

01:25:34.140 --> 01:25:35.940
that we might think we might know,

01:25:35.940 --> 01:25:38.010
are these Roman systems,

01:25:38.010 --> 01:25:40.290
something like republican principles

01:25:40.290 --> 01:25:43.800
of power relations among magistrates.

01:25:43.800 --> 01:25:47.770
That one, you can protect
yourself from errant magistrates

01:25:49.005 --> 01:25:51.055
by having them always be in even numbers.

01:25:53.790 --> 01:25:57.420
And that is a kind of principle
that they widely observe.

01:25:57.420 --> 01:25:59.850
So that's a feature of Roman public law

01:25:59.850 --> 01:26:01.680
that I think was probably
true before, yeah.

01:26:01.680 --> 01:26:05.043
And then it is noteworthy, I think,

01:26:07.410 --> 01:26:11.310
that a kind of interesting
number of Latin abstractions

01:26:11.310 --> 01:26:13.830
that derive from comparatives

01:26:13.830 --> 01:26:16.590
appear in the language
of government, right?

01:26:16.590 --> 01:26:19.350
Not simply maiestas, but
magisterium, ministerium,

01:26:19.350 --> 01:26:20.430
and all that.

01:26:20.430 --> 01:26:23.520
I mean, I don't know of
any other domain of Latin

01:26:23.520 --> 01:26:26.490
in which there's so many
abstractions from comparatives

01:26:26.490 --> 01:26:27.363
as government.

01:26:28.680 --> 01:26:29.880
And I think this,

01:26:29.880 --> 01:26:31.560
I think a lot of what the,

01:26:31.560 --> 01:26:36.180
a lot of how they organize
something like public law,

01:26:36.180 --> 01:26:38.760
which is to say moments of
dispute between magistrates,

01:26:38.760 --> 01:26:40.710
which is when something
would have to be settled,

01:26:40.710 --> 01:26:44.610
had to do with like two consuls facing off

01:26:44.610 --> 01:26:46.110
or having two people argue,

01:26:46.110 --> 01:26:47.670
and then somebody saying, "Well,
somebody's more important,

01:26:47.670 --> 01:26:49.030
somebody's less important."

01:26:49.030 --> 01:26:50.363
And that was it.

01:26:52.110 --> 01:26:54.510
And you know, there's a really, I mean,

01:26:54.510 --> 01:26:56.190
this is, I guess, part of what, I mean,

01:26:56.190 --> 01:26:58.740
we might talk about with Seth tomorrow.

01:26:58.740 --> 01:26:59.850
I might be taken to task.

01:26:59.850 --> 01:27:02.880
But if you look at something like

01:27:02.880 --> 01:27:07.640
Roman administration of Italy before 200,

01:27:11.460 --> 01:27:13.080
the demands of Roman administration

01:27:13.080 --> 01:27:15.360
for something like a
state knowledge apparatus

01:27:15.360 --> 01:27:17.040
were astonishingly tiny.

01:27:17.040 --> 01:27:18.810
Just astonishingly tiny.

01:27:18.810 --> 01:27:20.943
I mean, you know, there was,

01:27:22.500 --> 01:27:23.430
I mean, if you name something

01:27:23.430 --> 01:27:24.810
that might actually have existed

01:27:24.810 --> 01:27:27.300
as opposed to stuff for which
we have virtually no evidence,

01:27:27.300 --> 01:27:30.000
the formula togatorum, right?

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:32.370
They didn't tax all these allies.

01:27:32.370 --> 01:27:34.230
You didn't need to keep
records of property

01:27:34.230 --> 01:27:35.670
or their riches or whatever.

01:27:35.670 --> 01:27:38.100
All the kind of later
stuff, all the, right,

01:27:38.100 --> 01:27:40.020
the form of later governmental knowledge,

01:27:40.020 --> 01:27:42.300
the application of the
census, of all things.

01:27:42.300 --> 01:27:44.610
I mean, the census with
its extraordinary ideology

01:27:44.610 --> 01:27:47.580
as an instrument for assessing
who is a citizen and not.

01:27:47.580 --> 01:27:49.830
The moment when you start
applying the census to foreigners

01:27:49.830 --> 01:27:52.470
is an extraordinary ideological moment.

01:27:52.470 --> 01:27:53.640
But it has a minor thing to do

01:27:53.640 --> 01:27:54.740
with the fiscal demands of the state

01:27:54.740 --> 01:27:59.310
and the way in which we're
now taxing people, right?

01:27:59.310 --> 01:28:04.050
But before, you know, before some date,

01:28:04.050 --> 01:28:09.050
the Roman demands on subjects
of Rome, especially in Italy,

01:28:09.060 --> 01:28:10.680
were amazingly primitive.

01:28:10.680 --> 01:28:12.180
And I think, I mean, by primitive,

01:28:12.180 --> 01:28:13.013
I mean their,

01:28:14.070 --> 01:28:18.573
the knowledge needs of these
processes were incredibly few.

01:28:20.059 --> 01:28:22.110
I mean that's, to come back to public law.

01:28:22.110 --> 01:28:26.470
I mean, their later public
documents of the state

01:28:27.360 --> 01:28:30.360
which are co-related to functions, right?

01:28:30.360 --> 01:28:32.130
In general, in spite of Andrew Riggsby,

01:28:32.130 --> 01:28:34.200
the Roman state didn't go
nuts for writing shit down

01:28:34.200 --> 01:28:35.640
unless it needed the knowledge,

01:28:35.640 --> 01:28:37.050
or unless having the knowledge

01:28:37.050 --> 01:28:39.330
had some really symbolic relationship

01:28:39.330 --> 01:28:40.710
to an important function of government,

01:28:40.710 --> 01:28:42.840
regardless of whether
it was actually used.

01:28:42.840 --> 01:28:45.992
But the Roman state in the
third century, what knowledge,

01:28:45.992 --> 01:28:47.430
knowledge was not,

01:28:47.430 --> 01:28:50.670
knowledge was not an instrument of power.

01:28:50.670 --> 01:28:54.630
I think in the, you know,
in the early one state.

01:28:54.630 --> 01:28:56.051
Now Seth doesn't believe me at all.

01:28:56.051 --> 01:28:56.884
(audience laughs)

01:28:56.884 --> 01:28:59.820
Now I've really, I tried
in writing the paper.

01:28:59.820 --> 01:29:01.470
I knew, particularly, you know,

01:29:01.470 --> 01:29:06.420
particularly with Chris being
here, and, you know, and Seth,

01:29:06.420 --> 01:29:07.740
I really didn't wanna say something stupid

01:29:07.740 --> 01:29:09.540
about early republican Latin,

01:29:09.540 --> 01:29:13.260
or, for that matter, about
the Roman state before 200.

01:29:13.260 --> 01:29:14.649
But now I've gone and done it, so...

01:29:14.649 --> 01:29:15.930
(audience laughs)

01:29:15.930 --> 01:29:17.880
- So you can see the
conversation's not over,

01:29:17.880 --> 01:29:20.550
as has been alluded to a thousand times.

01:29:20.550 --> 01:29:23.342
We'll continue tomorrow
morning, maybe at 9:00 AM,

01:29:23.342 --> 01:29:25.009
in Phelps Hall, 401.

01:29:25.980 --> 01:29:29.250
And we have three distinguished
speakers in our company,

01:29:29.250 --> 01:29:31.320
among whom is Seth Bernard.

01:29:31.320 --> 01:29:33.450
He's been alluded to and pointed at,

01:29:33.450 --> 01:29:34.283
mentioned a number of times.

01:29:34.283 --> 01:29:35.176
(audience laughs)

01:29:35.176 --> 01:29:37.440
A major expert in republican Roman history

01:29:37.440 --> 01:29:39.390
who's joining us from the
University of Toronto.

01:29:39.390 --> 01:29:42.000
And we also have Emily Hurt,

01:29:42.000 --> 01:29:46.200
who is joining us from John
Cabot University in Rome

01:29:46.200 --> 01:29:48.780
as a recent PhD of this program,

01:29:48.780 --> 01:29:52.050
but also has tremendous knowledge
of the Republican period.

01:29:52.050 --> 01:29:53.370
And finally, Rafail Zoulis,

01:29:53.370 --> 01:29:56.340
current graduate student
who's studying Roman law.

01:29:56.340 --> 01:29:57.540
More in the imperial period,

01:29:57.540 --> 01:29:59.160
but I think has lots to contribute, too.

01:29:59.160 --> 01:30:00.720
All these people will comment on

01:30:00.720 --> 01:30:04.110
and then discuss the
paper with Professor Ando.

01:30:04.110 --> 01:30:05.850
Please join us, if you will.

01:30:05.850 --> 01:30:10.470
And in the meantime, though,
I had promised riches.

01:30:10.470 --> 01:30:12.330
We haven't seen that
riches were delivered.

01:30:12.330 --> 01:30:13.980
The good thing about riches
is they pay dividends

01:30:13.980 --> 01:30:15.990
in the form of food and drink.

01:30:15.990 --> 01:30:18.540
We can go over to the Union League

01:30:18.540 --> 01:30:23.540
and the above, the second
story party room, as it were,

01:30:24.300 --> 01:30:26.310
and continue the conversation

01:30:26.310 --> 01:30:28.530
over some nice nibbles and drinks.

01:30:28.530 --> 01:30:30.555
But first, let's give our
speaker a round of applause.

01:30:30.555 --> 01:30:33.555
(audience applauds)

01:30:39.055 --> 01:30:41.805
(peaceful music)

